1601:
** ἐκπίπτω**,
_future_ -πεσοῦμαι: _aorist_ ἐξέπεσον: _perfect_ ἐκπέπτωκα:— **fall out of,** δίφρου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.585; ἵππων 11.179; ἀντύγων ἄπο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1193, etc.: with _dative_ _person_, τόξον δέ οἱ ἔκπεσε χειρός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.465}}; θαλερὸν δέ οἱ ἔκπεσε δάκρυ **fell from** his eyes, {{Refs|2.266: _absolutely_}}**fall out,** {{Refs|23.467}}; **fall down,** of trees, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.2.7; οἱ λεγόμενοι ἀστέρες ἐκπίπτειν}}**meteors,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.54U. —After 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, in various relations, frequently as}}_passive_ of ἐκβάλλω:
**1** of seafaring men, **to be cast ashore,** ἐκ δ᾽ ἔπεσον θυμηγερέων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.283; ἐ. τῇσι νηυσὶ ἐς Ἰηπυγίην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.138; πρὸς τὰς πέτρας [prev. author] 8.13; πρὸς πέτραις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1211}}; ναυαγὸν ἐ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 539; ἐ. πρὸς τὴν χώραν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 866d}}; of things, **suffer shipwreck,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.5.13}}; of fish, **to be cast up,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 601b32.}}
**2** **fall from** a thing, i.e. **be deprived of** it, ἐκ πολλῶν καὶ εὐδαιμόνων ἐς πτωχηΐην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.14, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 1.1}}; τυραννίδος, ἀρχῆς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 756, 757; [ἀπὸ] τῶν ἐλπίδων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.81; ἐκ τῆς δόξης 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.64; τῶν ὑπαρχόντων 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.51 W.}}
**3** **to be driven out,** [ἐκ τῆς ἀκροπόλιος] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.72}}; **to be banished,** ἐ. ἐκ τῆς πατρίδος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.150, compare 6.121; ἐ. χθονός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 766, compare “Aj.” 1177; ἐ. πολέμῳ ἢ στάσει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.2; γυμνὸς θύραζ ἐξέπεσον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 244}}; ὑπό τινος by a person, ἐκ Πελοποννήσου ὑπὸ Μήδων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.141; ὑπὸ τοῦ πλήθους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.66, compare “Inscription Prien.” 37.71; πρός τινος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 948, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 679:—in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.50 the preposition ἐς is corrupt.}}
**4** of limbs, **to be dislocated,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 8, etc.}}; of flesh, **mortify and separate itself,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Fract.” 27}}; so ἐ. ὀδόντες, πτερά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 745b6, “HA” 519a26, etc.}}; of atoms, ἐκπεσοῦσαι κατέψυξαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 60.}}
**5** **go forth, sally out,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.74; ἐκ τοῦ σταυρώματος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.4.11: _absolutely_, [prev. author] “An.” 5.2.17}}; of rays, **issue forth,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor Aphr. “de An.” 127.31.}}
**6** **come out,** of votes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 5.10}}; **turn out, happen,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 70.27, +others}}
**7** **escape,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.95.}}
**8** of oracles, **issue,** χρησμὸς ἐκπίπτει {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 43, etc.; ἐκπεσεῖν φωνὴν ἐξ ἄλσους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 9}}; **to be published, become known,** εἰς ἀνθρώπους ἀπαιδεύτους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 314a; φήμη ἐ. ἐς τοὺς Ἕλληνας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cleomenes” 5: _absolutely_, ἀπόκρισις ἐ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.32.10.}}
**9** **depart,** ἐκ τῆς ὁδοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.31; ἐκ τοῦ ἐπιτηδεύματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 495a.}}
**9.b** **digress,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.88; ἐ. ἐκ τοῦ λόγου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.34}}; but ἐ. τῆς διανοίας **miss** the sense, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 7.26}}; **fall outside of** a class, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “de Anima liber - pars” 169.17.}}
**10** of things, **escape** one **unawares,** φασὶν ἐκπεσεῖν αὐτούς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1111a9, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 8; ἐ. τὴν αἴσθησιν 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in librum de sensu commentarium - pars” 147.18}}; of reason, **fail, be lacking,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1202a3.}}
**11** **degenerate,** εἰς ἀλλότριον ἦθος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 497b}}; εἰς τὴν Φρυγιστὶ ἁρμονίαν **slip** into.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1342b11}}: _absolutely_, **come to naught,** {{NT|NT.Rom.9.6}}; **to be dilapidated,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.204.74.}}
**12** of actors or dramatic pieces, **to be hissed off the stage,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.265, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1456a18, 1459b31}}: so of orators, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 517a, compare “Phlb.” 13d.}}
**13** ἐ. ἑαυτοῦ **lose** one's self-control, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.36}}; ἐ. σκοποῦ **miss** the mark, {{Refs|[prev. work] 8.7.}}
**14** of things, **arise from,** ἔκ τινος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 136.3.}}
**15** of money, **cease to be current,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 7.303.14 (Oropus, 3rd c.BC).}}
**16** **run to excess,** δι᾽ ἀοριστίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 63; [ὁ πλοῦτος] εἰς ἄπειρον ἐ. [prev. author] “Sent.” 15, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JConf.” 7.}}
**16.b** Geometry texts, as _passive_ of ἐκβάλλω, **to be produced,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἑλίκων” 14.}}
**17** **die,** χθὼν ἐκπιπτόντων {{Refs|“Not.Scav.” 1923.35}} (unless, = **rubbish** heap).

1602:
** ἐκπλέω**,
_future_ -πλεύσομαι: _perfect_ - πέπλευκα {{Refs|“IG” 2.793a7}}: _Ionic dialect_ **ἐκπλώω**, _aorist_ -έπλωσα: _perfect_ πέπλωκα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 1084}}:—**sail out** or **away,** τοῦ Πόντου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.5; ἔξω τοῦ Ἑλλησπόντου [prev. author] 5.103; τῆσδ᾽ ἐ. χθονός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1375}}; ἐκ τῆσδε γῆς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 577; ἐ. ἐς ἀποικίην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.22}}; κατ᾽ Εὐρώπης ζήτησιν, κατὰ ληΐην, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.44, 152}}; ἐπί τινα against.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.37}}; of fish, **swim out,** ἀγεληδὸν ἐ. ἐς θάλασσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.93.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, ἐκπλεῖν τῶν φρενῶν **go out of** one's mind, **lose** one's senses, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 3.155, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Fragmenta” 240.}}
**II** rarely with _accusative_ loci, **sail out past,** τὸ ἔθνος τῶν Ἰχθυοφάγων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Indica” 29.7, compare 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 1084, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.645.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἐ. τὸν ὕστερον ἔκπλουν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.6.}}
**III** _transitive_, ἐ. ἐς τὴν εὐρυχωρίαν τὰς τῶν πολεμίων ναῦς **outsail** them into the open sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.102 (assuming variant).}}

1603:
** ἐκπληρ-όω**,
**fill up,** ἑκατὸν ἐχίδναις ἀσπίδ᾽ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1135.}}
**2** **make up** to a certain number, ἐκπληροῦσι τὰς ἴσας μυριάδας ἐκείνοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.186; ἐξεπληροῦτο τὸ ναυτικὸν ἐς τὰς..τριηκοσίας ναῦς [prev. author] 8.82}}; δέκατον ἐκπληρῶν ὄχον **making up the number of** ten chariots, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 708; ἐ. τοὺς ἱππέας εἰς δισχιλίους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.24.}}
**3** **man completely,** τριήρεις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1327b14.}}
**4** **fulfil,** ἡ χάρις ἐκπεπλήρωται {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.144; μοῖραν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 1.5; εὐαγγελίαν NT.Act.13.33.}}
**5** **pay off,** τὸ χρέος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 958b.}}
**II** ἐ. λιμένα πλάτῃ **make one's way over,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 54.}}

1604:
** ἐκπλήρ-ωσις**, εως, ἡ,
**filling up,** Apollon.Cit.I; κλεψύδρας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Marcellinus Medicus “de Pulsibus - Festschrift zur 49. Versammlung deutscher Philologen und Schulmänner” 265}}; **completion,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Aesara Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.49.27, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.58; ἐνιαυτοῦ 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.46}}; **filling up the measure,** ἁμαρτιῶν {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.6.14}}; **satisfaction,** τῶν ἐπιθυμιῶν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.86, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.567.}}
**II** **fulfilment** of a cosmic cycle, **Cat. Codex Astr.**{{Refs|1.163.}}

1605:
** ἐκπλήσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **ἐκπλήττω**, **strike out of, drive away from, expel,** ἐκ δ᾽ ἔπληξέ μου τὴν αἰδῶ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 134}}; ὃς (i.e. κεραυνὸς) αὐτὸν ἐξέπληξε τῶν.. κομπασμάτων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 362, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 635}}: _absolutely_,**drive away,** ἡ τέρψις τὸ λυπηρὸν ἐκπλήσσει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.38; φόβος μνήμην ἐ. [prev. work] 87.}}
**II** **drive out of one's senses by a sudden shock, amaze, astound,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.231 (tmesis); κάλλει καὶ ὥρᾳ διενεγκόντες ἐ. τινάς 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.134; ὁ φόβος ἐκπλήσσων.. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.7; κακοὶ εὐτυχοῦντες ἐκπλήσσουσί με “Tragica Adespota” 465}}; ὅ μ᾽ ἐκπλήσσει λόγου **frightens** me in speaking, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 549:}} — in this sense most frequently in _aorist 2_ _passive_, _Epic dialect_ ἐξεπλήγην (see. below), _Attic dialect_ ἐξεπλάγην [ᾰ] (also _aorist_ I ἐξεπλήχθην {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 183}}: _perfect_ _participle_ ἐκπεπληγμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 290, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 386, etc.)}}; **to be panic-struck, amazed,** especially by fear, ἐκ γὰρ πλήγη φρένας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.403, compare 13.394; ἡνίοχοι ἔκπληγεν 18.225}}: with _participle_, ἐκπεπληγμένον κεῖνον βλέποντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 922, compare “Ant.” 433, etc.}}; ἐκπλαγῆναί τινι **to be astonished at** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116, etc.; ὑπό τινος [prev. author] 3.64; διά τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.21; ἐπί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.27; πρός τι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 19, etc.: also with _accusative_, ἐκπλαγῆναί τινα}} **to be struck with panic fear of.**., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 226, “El.” 1045; ἡμᾶς δ᾽ ἂν..μάλιστα ἐκπεπληγμένοι εἶεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.11, compare 3.82.}}
**II.2** generally, of any sudden, overpowering passion, **to be struck with desire,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 673}}; **with love,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 38, “Med.” 8}}; χαρᾷ, ἡδονῇ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 233, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 629}}; **with admiration,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.148, etc.: c._accusative_ _of things_, ἐκπλαγέντα τὰ προκείμενα ἀγαθά [prev. author] 9.82.}}
**II.3** εἰς ὁμολογίαν ἐκπλήττειν **frighten** one into.., _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.4.11.}}

1606:
** ἐκ-πνέω**,
_Epic dialect_ **ἐκ-πνείω** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.349}}, _imperfect_ -είεσκον {{Refs|[prev. author] 13.148:}}_future_ -πνεύσομαι or -οῦμαι:— **breathe out** or **forth,** κεραυνὸς ἐκπνέων φλόγα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 361; ἐ. ἀράς τινι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 876; ἐ. θυμόν [prev. author] “Ba.” 620: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 100.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 112b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 492b6.}}
**2** βίον ἐ. **breathe one's last, expire,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1493 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 142; ἐ. ψυχήν [prev. author] “Or.” 1163; alone, ὑφ᾽ οὗ φονέως ἄρ᾽ ἐξέπνευσας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1026; πρός τινος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 886 (_anapaest meter_): _absolutely_, [prev. author] “Hyps.fragment” 60i38, 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 4.6}}: _metaphorically_, **lose power,** {{Refs|Gp. 15.1.28}}; **lose lustre,** of pearls, {{Refs|“PHolm.” 10.18.}}
**3** **lose breath,** of a runner, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1409a32.}}
**II** _absolutely_, **cease blowing, become calm,** [ὁ δῆμος] ἴσως ἂν ἐκπνεύσειε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 700}}; τὰ κατὰ τὸν πόλεμον ἐκπέπνευκε καὶ λελώφηκεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 942.}}
**II.2** **blow out** or **outwards,** of a wind, ἔσωθεν ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.36; ἐκ τοῦ κόλπου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.84, compare 6.104}}; **burst out,** σμικροῦ νέφους..ἐκπνεύσας μέγας χειμών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1148}}; but simply, **blow,** of wind, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 365a4, “Pr.” 947a31.}}

1608:
** ἐκπορνεύω**,
**commit fornication,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.38.24, NT.Jude.7}}:—_passive_, in same sense, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 6.126.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, of idolatry, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.34.15.}}
**II** with _accusative_, **prostitute,** τὴν θυγατέρα{{LXX|LXX.Lev.19.29}}; **cause to commit fornication,**{{LXX|LXX.2Ch.21.11.}}

1609:
** ἐκπτύω**,
_future_ -ύσομαι [ῠ] {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.196 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus)}}:— **spit out,** στόματος δ᾽ ἐξέπτυσεν ἅλμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.322, compare “Anthologia Graeca” l}}.with; of the sea, **cast up,**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 6.224 (3rd c.BC: Theodoridas Epigrammaticus): _metaphorically_, ὥσπερ χαλινὸν τὸν λόγον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.328c}}; so, of a ligature, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 45.24.7 (_passive_)}}; **spit** or **blab out,** ἀπόρρητα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 4.44}}; of an abortion, {{Refs|[prev. work] 12.17 (_passive_).}}
**II** **spit** in token of disgust, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 792.}}
**II.2** **spit at, abominate,** {{NT|NT.Gal.4.14.}}

1611:
** ἔκστᾰσις**, εως, ἡ,
(ἐξίστημι) **displacement,** ἄρθρων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 56; πᾶσα κίνησις ἔ. ἐστι τοῦ κινουμένου 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 406b13}}: hence, **change,** εἰς ἀντικείμενα {{Refs|[prev. author] “GA” 768a27; αἱ κακίαι ἐ. [prev. author] “Ph.” 247a3; ἔ. ἐστιν ἐν τῇ γενέσει τὸ παρὰ φύσιν τοῦ κατὰ φύσιν [prev. author] “Cael.” 286a19; ἔ. τῆς φύσεως}}**degeneracy,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.1.6}}; opposed to στάσις, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.3.2}}; **movement outwards,** ἔ. ἀπὸ τοῦ παράγοντος {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 97}} bis; ἔ. εἰς τὸ ἔξω{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. work] 401; [σῶμα] ἐν ἐκστάσει λαβὸν τὴν ὑπόστασιν 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 36}}; **differentiation,** ἔ. καὶ πιῆθος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.7.17; αἱ εἰς πλῆθος ἐ. 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Timaeum commentarii” 2.203 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**II** **standing aside,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1361a37 (_plural_).}}
**II.b** = Latin _cessio bonorum_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“CPR” 20ii9 (3rd c.AD)}}; ἔ. χρημάτων {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.53}}; **a tax on cessions**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 914.6 (2nd c.AD), “PLond.” 2.305.2 (“PTeb.” ii p.184).}}
**II.2** **distraction** of mind, from terror, astonishment, anger, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 7.5, “Prorrh.” 2.9; ἔ. σιγῶσα [prev. author] “Coac.” 65; ἔ. μανική 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 10a1; ἔ. τῶν λογισμῶν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 8; νοῦ 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.3.7; τὰ μηδὲ προσδοκώμεν᾽ ἔκστασιν φέρει 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 149, compare “Epit.” 472, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 113; εἰς ἔ. ἄγειν 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 1.4.}}
**II.3** **entrancement, astonishment,** {{NT|NT.Luke.5.26, NT.Mark.5.42.}}
**II.4** **trance,** {{NT|NT.Acts.10.10, 22.17}}; **ecstasy,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.9.11; ἔ. καὶ μανία 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.103A.}}
**II.4.b** **drunken excitement,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 30.}}

1612:
** ἐκστρέφω**,
**turn out of,** βόθρου τ᾽ ἐξέστρεψε [δένδρον] **rooted up** a tree **from** the trench it stood in, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.58.}}
**II** **turn inside out,** τὰ βλέφαρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 721}}: _metaphorically_, **change** or **alter entirely,** τοὺς τρόπους {{Refs|[prev. author] “Nu.” 88; τοὺς ἡμετέρους Ἱππέας [prev. work] 554:}} —_passive_, ποσὶν ἐξεστραμμένοις πορευόμενοι with feet **turned outwards,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 813a14}}; **to be distorted,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.27.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_ in _perfect_ _participle_ _passive_, γενεὰ ἐξεστραμμένη **perverse** generation, {{LXX|LXX.Deu.32.20.}}
**II.3** **transmute** base metal, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Zosimus Epigrammaticus Alch. p.195B.}}

1613:
** ἐκτᾰρ-άσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **ἐκταράττω**, **throw into confusion,** τοὺς ἵππους {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 7.4, etc.}}; **agitate,** τὸν δῆμον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 19, compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.97d}}:—_passive_, **to be greatly troubled, be confounded,** ὑπό τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.5, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.552f; πρός τι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Somn.” 16.}}
**II** in _passive_ also, **to have a bowel-complaint,** κοιλίη ἐκταραχθεῖσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.60, “Epid.” 1.15.}}

1614:
** ἐκτείνω**,
_future_ - τενῶ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 325, etc.}}:—**stretch out,** χεῖρ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἐκφορᾷ νεκροῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ch.” 9; τὴν χ. ὑπτίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 782}}; τὰς χεῖρας ἐπί τι for something, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.3.6}}; πρός τινα, in sign of friendship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.47.2; πρὸς κέντρα κῶλον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 325; παῖδας ἐπὶ τὴν πυρήν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.107; ἐκεῖσε κἀκεῖσ᾽ ἀσπίδ᾽ ἐ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1131; εἰς ἧπαρ ξίφος [prev. author] “Ph.” 1421}}: _absolutely_, **offer food,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.186c}}; τὰ γόνατ᾽ ἐ. **straighten** the knees, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1212; ἐ. τὰ σκέλη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.14; νοῦ ἄπο μυρίον ὄμμα “IG” 3.716; ἐ. νέκυν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 786}}; ἓν γὰρ ἐκτενεῖ σ᾽ ἔπος **will lay** thee **prostrate,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Med.” 585}}:— _passive_, **to be outstretched, lie at length,** of sleepers, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 858 (Lyric poetry); ἐκταθεὶς ὥσπερ Ὀδυσσεὺς ἀφικέσθαι εἰς τὴν Ἑλλάδα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.1.2, etc.}}; of countries, etc., **extend,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Vect.” 4.3, 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 40.}}
**2** **stretch, spread out** a net, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 991}}; **extend the line of** an army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 801, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 5.6}}; λαὸν ἐκτείνοντ᾽ ἄνω (i.e. ἑαυτόν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 654; στράτευμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.19}}:—_passive_, **to be unfolded, smoothed,** ὡς ἂν Διὸς μέτωπον ἐκταθῇ χαρᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 902.}}
**II** **spin out, prolong,** πλεῦνα λόγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.51; φροίμιον θεοῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 829}}; μακρὰν ἐξέτεινας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 916, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1351; μῆκος λόγου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 201; μείζονα λόγον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 679, etc.; βίον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1109; τοὺς περιπάτους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.13.5}}:—_passive_, λόγος ἐκταθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 887a}}; of Time, πολὺς ἐκτέταται χρόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1402 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**III** **put to the full stretch,** ἵππον ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.5; ἐ. πάντα κάλων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 338a}}; πᾶσαν προθυμίην ἐ. **put forth** all one's zeal, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10. η; τὸν θυμόν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.31; ἅπασαν ἀγωνίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.30}}: _metaphorically_ in _passive_, **to be on the rack,** ἐκτέταμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 153 (Lyric poetry).}}
**IV** **lengthen** a short syllable, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 27.2 (_passive_), +others, interpolated in 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.58.}}
**V** _intransitive_, **draw along,** {{LXX|LXX.Judg.20.37.}}

1615:
** ἐκτελ-έω**,
_Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ἐξετέλειον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.493, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.7}}: _Epic dialect_ _future_ - τελέω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.286, 10.105}}: _aorist_ _participle_ ἐκτελέσσαντες {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 6.5:}}_future_ _middle_ in _passive_ sense (see. below):—**bring to an end, accomplish, achieve,** ἐκτελέσας μέγα ἔργον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.275; ὥς κεν..ἐκτελέσειεν ἀέθλους 8.22; ὁδὸν ἐκτελέσαντες 10.41, etc.}}; **fulfil** a promise, etc., οὐδέ τοι ἐκτελέουσιν ὑπόσχεσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.286; μή οἱ ἀπειλὰς ἐκτελέσωσιθεοί 9.245; οὔ θην Ἕκτορι πάντα νοήματα..Ζεὺς ἐκτελέει 10.105, etc.; ἐπιθυμίην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; ἔρωτα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 193c; τἀντεταλμένα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1648 codices; μυστήρια “PMag. Osl.” 1.306: _absolutely_, Δαρείου ἐκτελέσας (i.e. τὸ ἔργον) κατὰ νοῦν epigram cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.88}}:—_passive_, ὧδε γὰρ ἐκτελέεσθαι ὀΐομαι **will be accomplished,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.217, compare 7.353; ἐκτελοῖτο δὴ τὰ χρηστά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 228.}}
**2** of Time, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 565, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.69, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.104}}:—_passive_, μῆνές τε καὶ ἡμέραι ἐξετελεῦντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.294.}}

1616:
** ἐκτέν-εια**, ἡ,
**zeal, assiduousness,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Molpis 3, “PPetr.” 3p.18 (3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.2, etc.}}; **'gush', 'empressement',** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 10.17.1; ἐ. καὶ φιλοτιμία “IG” 22.1343.28, cf. Hierocl.p.62A.; τὰν πᾶσαν ἐ. καὶ κακοπαθίαν παρεχόμενος “IG” 12(1).1032.10 (from Carpathos), compare “Inscription Prien.” 107.20, +others (2nd c.BC), “UPZ” 110.12 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; ἐν ἐκτενείᾳ **eagerly,** {{LXX+NT|NT.Acts.26.7, cf. LXX.Jdt.4.9}}; μετὰ πάσης ἐ.{{LXX|LXX.2Mac.14.38.}}
**II** **abundance,** ξύλων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.2.4, compare 8.2.6.}}
**III** **extension,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 65.}}

1617:
** ἐκτεν-ής**, ές,
**strained**: hence of persons, **warmly attached, friendly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.22.4 (_superlative_),compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 34.2.39, 5th-4th c.BC: Socratis et Socraticorum Epistulae cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.31.130}}; **assiduous,** περί τινα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 2.277.5 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}: _comparative_ - έστερος τῇ προθυμίᾳ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IGRom.” 4.293a ii 38 (Pergam., 2nd c.BC)}}: _superlative_ - εστάτη προθυμία {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.293, compare “Vita Philonidis Epicurei” p.9C.; πρόνοια “UPZ” 110.46 (2nd c.BC).}}
**2** **extended,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 64}}; **capable of extension,** ἐ. ἐστι τὸ μεταδίδον τῶν ἑαυτοῦ καὶ τοῖς ἄλλοις {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.121A.}}
**3** **abundant,** γάλα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.94.}}
**II** _adverb_ -νῶς (Elean ἐκτενέωρ {{Refs|“GDI” 1172.12; Cretan dialect ἐκτενίως [prev. work]5138.13}} _Ionic dialect_ -έως Ps.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus “Vitae Homeri” 7)}} **earnestly, zealously,** ἀγαπᾶσθαι Machoap.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.579e; ποιεῖν τι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1210a27; συναγωνίζεσθαι “IG” 22.945, compare “SIG” 538.17 (Delph., 3rd c.BC)}}; εὐχὴ ἐ. γινομένη {{NT|NT.Act.12.5}}: _comparative_ -έστερον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 13.9.1}}: _superlative_ -έστατα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 29.4.}}
**II.2** in _adverb_ also, **eagerly, freely, splendidly** (condemned by {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 285), προσδέξασθαί τινα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.19.1, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.24, etc.}}; of public duties, λαμπρῶς καὶ ἐ. τετελεκότα {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2771ii14 (from Aphrodisias)}}: _comparative_, πολυτελῶς καὶ ἐκτενέστερον τῶν ἄλλων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus “Fragmenta Historica - FHG” 6J.}}—Not in early writers, corrupt in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 983.}}

