1620:
** ἐκτίθημι**
(_infinitive_ -τιθεῖν{{Refs|“IG” 7.235.41)}}_future_ -θήσω: _perfect_ **-τέθεικα**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 62.4 (2nd c.BC)}}:—**set out, place outside**, πυκινὸν λέχος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.179}}; **expose** on a desert island, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 5}}; **expose** a new-born child, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.112, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 531, etc.; τὸν παῖδ᾽.. ἐξέθηκε δωμάτων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 344:}} —so in _passive_, τέθνηκε.. θηρσὶν ἐκτεθείς{{Refs|[prev. work] 951}}; **expose**, ἑαυτὸν βέλεσι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Polemo Sophista “Cynegirus” 7:}} —_middle_, ἐκτίθεσθαι λείαν εἰς Βιθυνούς **export** it thither, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 29.}}
**II** **set up, offer for a prize**, λέβητας ἐκτιθεὶς φέρειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 378; ἆθλα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.9.4.}}
**II.b** **fix** or **grant** allowances, rates of pay, etc., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 5.498 (3rd c.BC), “PEdgar” 2.4 (_passive_), etc.}}
**II.2** **exhibit publicly, post up**, νόμους πρὸς τοὺς ἐπωνύμους Decrees cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.83, cf. Legal cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.23; ἔκθεμα “PPetr.” 2p.44 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὀνόματα εἰς στοάν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 577.28 (Milet., 3rd-2nd c.BC):}} —_passive_, ὅπως ἐκτεθῶσι [οἱ νόμοι] {{Refs|“IG” 22.487.6, etc.}}
**II.3** **expose for sale**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 46.14 (_passive_).}}
**III** **set forth, expound**, τὴν πρόθεσιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1437b35; κατὰ γένος 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Char.Praef.” 3}}:—also _middle_, λόγους καθόλου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1455b1; τὴν ἑαυτῆς ἐρημίαν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.18, etc.}}
**III.2** Philos., **predicate separate existence** of a thing, **explain by means of abstraction**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1086b10.}}
**IV** _middle_, **set forth, select** particular instances of a rule, [ποιεῖν τὴν ἀπόδειξιν] τῶ ἐκθέσθαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “APr.” 28a23:}} —_passive_, τὸ ἐκτεθέν{{Refs|[prev. work] 30a11.}}
**IV.2** **set out** terms in syllogistic form, {{Refs|[prev. work] 48a1, +others}}
**IV.3** **isolate** in thought, {{Refs|[prev. author] “SE” 179a3.}}
**IV.4** **pick out for separate treatment**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 235a28.}}
**IV.5** ἐ. καθόλου **set out** in general form, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Po.” 1455b1.}}

1621:
** ἐκτῐν-άσσω**,
**shake out**, in cleaning, ἔρια, ἱμάτια, {{Refs|“BGU” 827.22}}; **expel**, ἕλμινθας {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Diphilus Siphnius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.51f, compare 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.126; ἔμβρυα [prev. work]76:}} —_passive_, ἐκ δ᾽ ἐτίναχθεν ὀδόντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.348, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 14}}; [ὁ Φαέθων] ἐκτινάσσεται **is thrown out**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 52, compare 6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus 4.20.}}
**2** **shake off**, τὸν κονιορτὸν τῶν ποδῶν {{NT|NT.Matt.10.14, etc.}}:—_middle_, {{NT|NT.Act.13.51.}}
**3** **search thoroughly**, τοὺς βαδίζοντας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 37.5.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **make a disturbance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.2.19}}; **make a thorough search**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 5.12 (2nd c.BC)}}; **kick out**, of animals, εἰς τοὺς πλησίον ἵππους {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 19.2.}}

1622:
** ἐκτός**
(**ἐχθός**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 9(1).333 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC), “Michel” 995 C 35 (Delph.), etc.)}}, _adverb_, (ἐκ) **without, outside**, opposed to ἐντός:
**1** as _preposition_ with _genitive_, which may either precede or follow, ἐ. κλισίης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.13; τείχεος ἐ. 21.608}}; **out of, far from**, καπνοῦ καὶ κύματος ἐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.219; especially in proverbial phrases (see. ἔξω 1 near the end), ἐ. κλαυμάτων ἔχειν πόδα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1260}}; ἐ. ἔχειν πόδα (i.e. τῶν καλῶν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.289}}; ἐ. τῶν ἐλαῶν **beyond** the olives, i. e. out of the course. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 995 (Lyric poetry)}}; Geometry texts, **beyond**, τοῦ Α σημείου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 1.8, +others}}; also ἐ. ἀτασθαλίης **outside of, free from**.., {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 754, compare 744; ἐ. αἰτίης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.133, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 332, etc.; ἐ. πημάτων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 504; ἄτας [prev. author] “Ant.” 614 (Lyric poetry); τῶν κακῶν [prev. author] “fragment” 724, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 523b}}; ἐ. στρατειῶν **exempt from**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 498c}}; ἐ. ἑωυτῆς **beside** herself, **out of** her wits, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.90, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 640 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐ. ἐλπίδος **beyond** hope, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 330; ἡ ἐ. καὶ παρ᾽ ἐλπίδας χαρά, i.e. ἡ ἐκτὸς ἐλπίδων καὶ παρ᾽ ἐλπίδας, [prev. work] 392}}; δοκημάτων ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 771 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** of Time, **beyond**, πέντε ἡμερέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80.}}
**3** **except,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae [same place], etc.; ἐ. ὀλίγων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.2.3}}; **besides, apart from**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 474d, “PTeb.” 19.7 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.: _absolutely_, **besides, as well**, {{Refs|“GDI” 1742.12}}; also ἐ. εἰ μή **unless**, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.1Cor.15.2, 1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 7.8.1, Vett. Val. 37.20, +others, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 6; ἐ. ἐὰν μή “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 7.216; ἐ. ὅτι.. 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 10.5.}}
**4** **without the consent of**, τινός {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.356.}}
**II** _absolutely_, ἃ δ᾽ ἐ. **external things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 231 (Lyric poetry), compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.4.8, etc.}}; οἱ ἐ. **strangers, foreigners**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 629d, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.47.10, etc.}}; also, **the vulgar, the common herd**: **the Gentiles**, {{Refs|LXX “Si.prol.” 4.}}
**III** with Verbs of motion, ῥίπτειν ἐ. to throw **out**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 269; ᾖξας [prev. author] “El.” 1402 (Lyric poetry); ἐκπέμπειν [prev. author] “Ant.” 18; ἕλκειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 616a}}; οὐκ ἐ. ε; ={ἔξιθι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 676}}; χώρει ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1117}}; εἰ δ᾽ ἐ. ἔλθοις if thou **transgressest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1189.}}

1624:
** ἐκτρέπω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἐκ-τράπω** [ᾰ], **turn out of the course, turn aside**, τοῦ ποταμοῦ τὸ ῥέεθρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.186, compare 2.11, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.65; μηδ᾽ εἰς Ἑλένην κότον ἐκτρέψῃς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1464 (Lyric poetry), compare “Th.” 628 (Lyric poetry); τὸ δυστυχὲς δὲ τοῦτ᾽ ἐς ἄλλον ἐκτρέπει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 483}}; ἑαυτοῦ μιαρίαν εἴς τινα ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.9; ἐ. [τινὰ] πρὸς ποίμνας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 53}}:—_passive_ and _middle_, **turn off** o_ **aside**, ἐκτραπέσθαι ὁδὸν μακροτέρην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.104: _absolutely_, [prev. author] 2.80, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.22, etc.}}: with _genitive_, **turn aside from**, τοῦ πρόσθεν λόγου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 851; also ἐ. ἐκ.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.75; ἀπὸ.. ἐπί.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 222a; πόθεν δεῦρο ἐξετραπόμεθα [prev. author] “R.” 543c.}}
**2** **turn** a person **off the road, order** him **out of the way**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 806}}:—_passive_ (_future_ -τραπήσομαι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 86) and}}_middle_, ἐκτρέπεσθαί τινα **get out of** one's **way**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.225, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 837, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 5}}; **avoid**, τὸν ἔλεγχον {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 35.4.14; τὴν φιλοσοφίαν 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.223d}}: with _infinitive_, ὀφθῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 10.56.10 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus): _absolutely_, conjecture in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1541.}}
**3** τὴν δρῶσαν ἐ. **prevent** her **from** acting, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 350.}}
**4** ἀσπίδας θύρσοις ἐ. **turn** shields **and flee before** the thyrsus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 799.}}
**II** _middle_, **turn away**, φίλους {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 101}}; also ἐκτρέπεσθαι τὰ ἐντὸς ἐκτός **turns itself** inside out, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 621a7.}}
**III** in Medicine texts in _passive_, **to be diverted** or **everted**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Steril.” 213, “Off.” 14, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.15}} (perhaps **to be put out of joint,** {{NT|NT.Heb.12.13, “Hippiatrica” 26).}}
**IV** **turn** or **change**, εἴς ἄσπορον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PRyl.” 133.22 (1st c.AD),compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 14.28}}:—_passive_, εἰς ὀλιγαρχίαν ἐκτραπῆναι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.4.9; ὑπ᾽ ἀγεννείας εἰς μέμψεις 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.6.42.}}
**V** _passive_, **to be brought to birth**, Astrology texts technical term, Vett. Val.{{Refs|50.27, +others}}

1625:
** ἐκτρέφω**,
**bring up from childhood, rear up**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.122, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 750, etc.; ἐξέφυσε κἀξέθρεψέ με 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 827}}; ἐκτεθραμμένοι σκύμνοι λεόντων **true-bred**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1222}}; of plants, τὸ ἐκτρέφον τὴν ῥίζαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.193; ἐκτρέφει ἡ γῆ τὸ σπέρμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 17.10; ποταμοῦ πνεῦμα τραχύτερον ἐκθρέψαντος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.357d}}:—_middle_, **rear up for oneself**, τινά {{Refs|“hymnus ad Cererem” 166}}; ἤνεγκα κἀξέσωσα κἀξεθρεψάμην, says the παιδαγωγός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 13, compare “fragment” 387, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 929a}}:—_passive_, εἴ σοί τις υἱός ἐστιν ἐκτεθραμμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 796; ἐγένου τε καὶ ἐξετράφης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 50e, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.8.}}
**II** _middle_, of pregnant animals, **nourish**, [ζῷα] μεγάλα ἐντὸς ἐκθρέψωνται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 91d}}:—_active_, **bring to birth**, τὰ κυήματα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 773a34.}}

1627:
** ἐκφέρω**,
_future_ ἐξοίσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71: _Ionic dialect_}}_aorist_ ἐξήνεικα:—_passive_, ἐξοισθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 561:}}_future_ _middle_ ἐξοίσομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.49, 76}}:— **carry out of**, τινὰ πολέμοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.664, etc.; ὅπλα ἐκ μεγάρου ἐξενηνειγμένα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.37, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 779; ἐ. πεύκας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 599; γραμματεῖον [prev. author] “Nu.” 19; ἐξένεγκέ μοι τὴν κοπίδ᾽ ἔξω 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 332.}}
**2** **carry out** a corpse for burial, ἐξέφερον θρασὺν Ἕκτορα δάκρυ χέοντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.786, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.117, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.21 (_passive_), etc.}}; also, **cause death**, εἰ ὑπερβάλλουσιν ἀλγηδόνες, ἐξοίσουσι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.4.8.}}
**3** **carry away**, τρί᾽ ἄλεισα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.470, compare “Test.Epict.” 2.22, etc.}}; **carry off** as prize or reward, ἄεθλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.785:}} —more frequently in _middle_, τὠυτὸ (of a victory) ἐξενείκασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.103}}; κλέος, δόξαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 60, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.1, etc.}}; **accomplish**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.66.}}
**4** **carry ashore**, ἐπὶ Ταίναρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24, etc.}}; **cast ashore**, πόντου νιν ἐξήνεγκε.. κλύδων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 701}}:—_passive_, with _future_ _middle_, **come to land, be cast ashore**, ἐς τοὺς ἑωυτῶν ἐξοίσονται Hdt.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus 49, compare 76, 2.90.}}
**II** **bring forth**, in various senses:
**II.1** of women, ={φέρειν μέχρι τέλους}, **bring to the birth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 19; εἰς φῶς κύημα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 461c, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 577b23, +others}}; of plants, **bear** seed, {{Refs|[prev. author] “GA” 731a22}}; of the ground, **bear** fruit, Δήμητρος καρπὸν ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.193, 4.198.}}
**II.2** **bring about, accomplish**, μισθοῖο τέλος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.451; τὸ μόρσιμον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.61; κακίας μεγάλας ὥσπερ ἀρετὰς αἱ μεγάλαι φύσεις ἐ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 1}}:—_passive_, διὰ ἀνοήτων οὐδὲν ἂν καλῶς ἐξενεχθείη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.7.}}
**II.3** **publish, deliver**, χρηστήριον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.79; ἐ. λόγον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 741, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 236c, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 23; εἰς τοὺς Ἕλληνας τὰ τῆς πόλεως ἁμαρτήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.14}}; of public measures, **refer**, ἐξενεῖκαι ἐς τὸν δῆμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.5; ἐς πολύφημον ἐξενείκαντας [prev. author] 5.79}}; ἐ. προβούλευμα εἰς τὸν δῆμον **bring** a project of law before the people, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.4}} (so in _middle_, ἐκφέρεσθαι προβούλευμα εἰς τὴν ἐκκλησίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.125)}}: _absolutely_, frequently in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., ἡ δὲ βουλὴ ἐς τὸν δῆμον ἐξενεγκέτω ἐπάναγκες {{Refs|“IG” 12.76.61, compare 22.360.47}}; of authors, **publish** a work, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.74, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1447b17, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.10c, etc.:}} —_middle_, ἐκφέρεσθαι γνώμην **declare one's** opinion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.36}}:—_passive_, εἰς Ἕλληνας ἐξοισθήσεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 561.}}
**II.4** **produce, exhibit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.30}}; **display**, δείγματα εἰς φῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 788c, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.12; φανερῶς τὸ μῖσος εἴς τινας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.27.3; ἐ. τὴν ἰατρικὴν ἐπιστήμην 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.74.}}
**II.5** **disclose**, τι πρὸς τὸν μάγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71}}; τὴν ἀπάτην{{Refs|[prev. work] 74; τὴν ἐπιχείρησιν [prev. author] 8.132.}}
**II.6** **put forth, exert**, δύνασιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1012}}:—and in _middle_, μέγα τι σθένος ἁ Κύπρις ἐκφέρεται νίκας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 497 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.7** ἐ. πόλεμον **begin** war, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.21; ἐπί τινα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.56; πρός τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.1; τινί 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.36.4, etc.}}
**II.8** **show the marks of, betray, reproduce**, ἐκφέρουσι γὰρ μητρῷ᾽ ὀνείδη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 621.}}
**II.9** ὅρον ἐ. **produce** a definition, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1040b2}}; **express**, διάνοιαν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.26, +others}}; '**word**' a sentence, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 3 (_passive_), 7}}; **utter**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 94}}; **cite, adduce**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 142}}; πρὸς ἑαυτὸν ἐ. **soliloquize**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.5.}}
**II.9.b** **pronounce**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.94f; ὅταν μακρῶς ἐκφέρηται 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 15, compare 4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 1, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.5.17.}}
**II.10** **pay as indemnity**, δισχίλια τάλαντα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.27.5, etc.}}
**II.10.b** _passive_, of words, **to be formed**, κατὰ μίμησιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 220; ἐπιρρηματικῶς 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 175.28; διὰ τοῦ ε ¯ ἐ. [prev. work] 193.5.}}
**II.11** **exact**, ἀργύριον {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.15.20.}}
**III** _passive_, **to be carried beyond bounds**, ἔξω ὅρων ἐξενεχθὲν ἀκόντιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.4}}: mostly _metaphorically_, **to be carried away** by passion, ἀπαιδευσίᾳ ὀργῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.84, compare 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.127}}; πρὸς ὀργὴν ἐκφέρει **givest way** to passion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 628}}; ἐ. πρὸς αἰδῶ **is inclined** to feel respect, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 601 (Lyric poetry); λέγων ἐξηνέχθην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 425a; ἐξενεχθεὶς ὥστε κωμῳδοποιὸς γενέσθαι [prev. author] “R.” 606c; πρὸς τὸ ἄγριοι πολῖται γενέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.34}}; πάθος defined as ὁρμὴ ἐκφερομένη καὶ ἀπειθὴς λόγῳ {{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.92}}:—later in _active_, [θυμὸς] ἐ. τινὰ τοῦ λογισμοῦ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.21.}}
**IV** **bring to** one's end, **bring on** to the trail, εὖ δέ σ᾽ ἐκφέρει.. βάσις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 7}}; κινδυνεύει ὥσπερ ἀτραπός [τις] ἐκφέρειν ἡμᾶς [ἐν τῇ σκέψει] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 66b, compare “IG” 12.94.37:}} —_passive_, ἐξηνέχθην εἰς ἅπερ Πρωταγόρας λέγει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 386a.}}
**V** _intransitive_ (i.e. ἑαυτόν) **shoot forth** (before the rest), ὦκα δ᾽ ἔπειτα αἱ Φηρητιάδαο.. ἔκφερον ἵπποι· τὰς δὲ μέτ᾽ ἐξέφερον Διομήδεος.. ἵπποι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.376, compare 759}}; also, **to run away**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 3.4.}}
**V.2** **come to fulfilment**, ὁρᾷς τὰ τοῦδε.. ὡς ἐς ὀρθὸν ἐκφέρει μαντεύμᾰτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1424}}; **come to an end**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 824 (Lyric poetry).}}

1628:
** ἐκ-φεύγω**,
_future_ **-ξομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 157, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 189b}}, and **-ξοῦμαι** {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 432d}}:—**flee out** or **away, escape**: _absolutely_, ἐκφυγέειν μεμαώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.231, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 510, etc.; φεύγων ἐκφεύγειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.95.}}
**b** of persons accused, **to be acquitted**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 157.}}
**2** with _genitive_, **escape out of**, ἐξέφυγον πολιῆς ἁλὸς ἤπειρόνδε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.236; νούσου “epigram Gr.” 1041.9}}; of things, βέλος ἔκφυγε χειρός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.18: with _preposition_, ματρὸς ἐκ κόλπων “APl.” 4.182 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus).}}
**3** with _accusative_, **escape**, ἐξ αὖ νῦν ἔφυγες θάνατον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.362; κῆρας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.512; κακότητα 5.414; θανάτοιο τέλος 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 6; νοῦσον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.25; Σκύθας [prev. author] 6.40; τὴν πεπρωμένην 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 518; τὰν θεῶν νέμεσιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 518 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}
**3.b** simply, **to have escaped, to be beyond**, οὐ πολλὰ ἐκφεύγεις παιδιὰς ἔτη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 268e.}}
**3.c** of things, ἐκπεφεύγασιν γάμοι με {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1622; ἐκφύγοι τὰ πράγματ᾽ αὐτόν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.33, compare 19.123}}; ἐ. τὰς αἰσθήσεις **escape** one's sense, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 208}}; also, **escape** one's lips, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 213d: _absolutely_, ἐκφεύγει τἀμελούμενον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 111, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1090b21.}}
**3.d** ἐκφεύγοντες τὴν χιόνα τόποι places **free from** snow, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.55.7.}}
**3.e** Astron., of stars, **emerge** from the Sun's rays, **become visible**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Autolycus Astronomus 1.9, 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 13.9, etc.}}
**3.f** **pass over, omit**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Apollonius Citiensis Medicus 1.}}
**4** with _infinitive_ (with or without _Article_), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 235b}}; οὐκ ἐκφεύγει μὴ οὐκ εἶναι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 277e; τὸ μὴ ἕτερα εἶναι [prev. author] “Prm.” 147a; ἐ. τὸ ἀποθανεῖν [prev. author] “Ap.” 39a; μικρὸν ἐξέφυγε μὴ καταπετρωθῆναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.2}}; ἐκφεύξεται τὰ δύο **will not admit of** duality, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.8.9.}}

1631:
** ἐκφύω**,
**generate**: mostly of the male, **beget**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 437, 827, etc.}}; ὃς ἐξέφυσεν Ἀερόπης λέκτρων ἄπο Ἀγαμέμνον᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 391.}}
**2** rarely of the female, **bear**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 984.}}
**3** generally, **produce**, ἡ γῆ κατὰ καιρὸν ἐκφύουσα πάντα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 397a26; ἐ. κέρατα [prev. author] “HA” 611b13}}: _absolutely_, of seed, **germinate**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.154.}}
**II** _passive_, with _perfect_ and _aorist 2_ _active_, **to be engendered, born from**, κεφαλαὶ τρεῖς ἑνὸς αὐχένος ἐκπεφυυῖαι (_Epic dialect_ _perfect_ _participle_) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.40}}; πατρός, μητρὸς ἐκφῦναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 487, 1295, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 542}}; λάληα ἐκπεφυκός a **born** tattler, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 320.}}
**II.2** **grow**, of hair, μέχρις ἂν [αἱ τρίχες] ἐκφυῶσι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.407}}; **spring, take rise**, of muscles, {{Refs|[prev. work]18(2).981.}}
**III** _intransitive_ in _present_ _active_, ἕλκεα ἐκφύουσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.5.15, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 883b26.}}

1632:
** ἐκχέω**
(later**ἐκφων-χύνω** {{NT|NT.Matt.23.35 (_passive_)}}, etc., condemned by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pseudol.” 29)}}_future_ -χέω (see. χέω): _aorist 1_ ἐξέχεα (also _imperative_ ἔκχυσον {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}; _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ _middle_ ἐκχευάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.178}}: _perfect_ ἐκκέχῠκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 915}}:—**pour out**, properly of liquids, οἶνον {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.296; αἷμ᾽ ἐκχέας πέδοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 653, cf. NT.Matt.23.35 (_passive_)}}; ἀναίτιον αἷμα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 1181.5 (Jewish, 2nd c.BC)}}; πηγάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 941; δάκρυα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215e (_passive_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 6; ὁ οἶνος ἐκχεῖται}}**is spilt, ** {{NT|NT.Matt.9.17: _metaphorically_, (in _middle_) ταχέας δ᾽ ἐκχεύατ᾽ ὀϊστούς}} he **poured forth his** arrows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.3, 24.178; σοὶ.. δαίμονες.. ἐλπίδας ἐξέχεαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epigrammata - PLG” 7.4.}}
**b** **pour away**: hence, **spill**, a vessel, ποδάνιπτρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 306}}; τὸν χόα Men.[same place]:—_passive_, **to be drained**, εἰς [διώρυχα] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PRyl.” 154.18 (1st c.AD).}}
**2** of words, **pour forth, utter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 554; μολπάς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 773; πολλὴν γλῶσσαν ἐκχέας μάτην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 929, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1029 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** **pour out like water, squander, waste**, ὄλβον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 826; τὰ πάντα [prev. author] “Ch.” 520, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1291; πλοῦτον ἐξέχεεν εἰς δαπάνας “Anthologia Graeca” 9.367 (2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista); ἐ. τά τε αὑτοῦ καὶ ἑαυτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 553b}}; **spoil**, τὸ πᾶν σόφισμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 13.}}
**4** **spread out**, λίνα, ὀθόνας, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.902 (tmesis), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 6.}}
**5** **throw down**, τινὰ κατὰ τοῦ κρηνοῦ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 13.8, compare 4.7, 14.10.}}
**6** ὕπνον ἐ. **shed**, i.e. **shake off** sleep, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.7.}}
**7** ={συγχέω},{ὅρκια} [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {ἐξέχεαν}.}}]
**II** _passive_, used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus mostly in}}_pluperfect_ ἐξεκέχυντο, as also in _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ ἐξέχῠτο or ἔκχῠτο, _participle_ ἐκχύμενος [ῠ]: later _future_ ἐκχῠθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 4.1}}:—**pour out, stream out** or **forth**, properly of liquids, {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.300, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.504, etc.; ἐκ δ᾽ ἄρα πᾶσαι χύντο χαμαὶ χολάδες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.525; so ἐξεχύθη τὰ σπλάγχνα NT.Act.1.18: _metaphorically_, of persons, σφήκεσσιν ἐοικότες ἐξεχέοντο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.259}}; ἱππόθεν ἐκχύμενοι **pouring from** the [wooden] horse, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.515; ἐκχυθέντες ἁλέες ἐκ τοῦ τείχεος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.13}}: generally, **to be spread out**, πολλὰ δὲ [δέσματα].. μελαθρόφιν ἐξεκέχυντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.279; σάρκες εἰς ὑπέρογκον ἐκκεχυμέναι πιότητα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 14.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, ῥηθέντα ματαίως ἐκκέχυται στομάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 39.3}}; **to be cast away, forgotten**, ἐκκέχυται φιλότης {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 110; αἱ πρόσθεν ὁμολογίαι ἐκκεχυμέναι εἰσίν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 49a.}}
**II.3** **give oneself up to any emotion, to be overjoyed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1469 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐ. εἰς ἑταίρας, εἰς τὸν κίνδυνον, **give oneself up to**.., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.25.4, 3.19.1}}; ἐπὶ τὰ εὐτρεπισθέντα, of a glutton, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.38; ἁβρὰ γελῶν ὄμμασιν ἐκκέχυσαι “Anthologia Graeca” 12.156.}}
**II.4** **lie languidly**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work]5.54.8 (3rd c.BC: Dioscorides Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.5** _metaphorically_, of Time, ἐ. κατὰ τὴν χρονικὴν παράτασιν {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 55.}}
**II.6** **extend**, of a piece of land, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“CPR” 1.8, +others (1st c.AD).}}

1633:
** ἐκχωρ-έω**,
**depart**, ἐκ χώρας {{Refs|“SIG” 679.53}}; **leave a country, emigrate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.56, 4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 30J.}}; **withdraw**, ἐκ τῆς οἰκίας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PAmh.” 2.30.44 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.: _metaphorically_, ἐ. ἐκ τοῦ ζῆν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.21.2: so _absolutely_, [prev. author] 7.2.1.}}
**2** **slip out of**, ἀστράγαλος ἐξεχώρησε ἐκ τῶν ἄρθρων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.129.}}
**3** **give way, retire**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 367, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 41.5; τῶν ὑπαίθρων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.15.7; τῶν ὑπαρχόντων [prev. author] 31.28.3; χειμῶνες ἐκχωροῦσιν εὐκάρπῳ θέρει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 671}}; ἐ. τινί τινος **give way** to a person in a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Jusj.”; τινὶ περί τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.20.1.}}
**4** _impersonal_ of a **motion** of the bowels, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.33.}}
**II** _transitive_, **give up, cede**, τινί τι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12(3).324.15 (from Thera), “PEleph.” 15.2 (3rd c.BC), “Sammelb.” 4414.8, etc.; τῷ δαίμονί τι Vett. Val. 156.4}}:—_passive_, {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4268 (from Xanthus).}}

1634:
** ἐκψύχω** [ῡ],
_future_ -ξω, **lose consciousness, swoon**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 1.5, LXX.Josh.4.21; ἐκ τάχα ψύξειν 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.29}}; **give up the ghost, expire**, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 115.11, NT.Act.5.5, 12.23.}}
**II** **to be short of breath, gasp**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 886b14.}}
**III** _passive_, **to be thoroughly cooled, chilled**,{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 882a36, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.695d.}}

1635:
** ἑκών**, ἑκοῦσα, ἑκόν:
(ϝέκ- {{Refs|“IG” 9(1).334.12 (_Locrian dialect_), “GDI” 5131b (from Crete), compare γεκαθά}}; cf. Sanskrit _váśmi_ 'wish'):—**readily**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.649, etc.; frequently contrasted with ἄκων, ἑ. ἀέκοντί γε θυμῷ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.43; οὐ γάρ τίς με βίῃ γε ἑ. ἀέκοντα δίηται 7.197; ἑκόνθ᾽ ἑκόντι Ζηνὶ συμπαραστατεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 220; πάρειμι δ᾽ ἄκων οὐχ ἑκοῦσιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 276; ἑκόντα μήτ᾽ ἄκοντα [prev. author] “Ph.” 771; βίᾳ τε κοὐχ ἑκών [prev. author] “OC” 935}}; ἑ. παρ᾽ ἑκόντος λαμβάνειν, i.e. by mutual consent, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.44; τὴν φύσιν ἑκοῦσαν καὶ οὐ παθοῦσαν τὰ δέοντα ποιεῖν 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.171.}}
**2** **wittingly, purposely**, ἑκὼν δ᾽ ἡμάρτανε φωτός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.372, etc.; σφόδρ᾽ ἑκὼν.. ἀγνοεῖν προσποιούμενος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.13.}}
**3** in _Attic dialect_ Prose {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare (2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 241), ἑ. εἶναι}} **as far as depends on one's will, as far as concerns one**, with a _negative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.104, 8.116, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 37a, +others}}; also in oblique cases, ὑπὸ σοῦ ἑκόντος εἶναι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 499c}}; or in a sentence implying a _negative_, θαυμάζοιμεν ἂν εἰ.. τις ἑκὼν εἶναι (perhaps delendum).. ἀφικνεῖτα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 646b: once affirmative, ἑκὼν εἶναι.. οἴχετο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.164.}}
**II** rarely of things, κακὰ ἑ. κοὐκ ἄκοντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1230.}}
**III** for _adverb_ see ἑκοντήν, ἑκοντί: regular _adverb_ ἑκόντως is uncertain in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.187, 226J.}}

1636:
** ἐλαία**,
_Attic dialect_ ἐλάα, ἡ, **olive-tree**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, especially in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, 11.590, +others}}; ἱερὴ ἐ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|. 13.372, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.13, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.55, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 701 (Lyric poetry), etc.; ἡμέρη ἐλαίη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.82 (opposed to ἀγρία ἐ. or κότινος) φέρεσθαι ἐκτὸς τῶν ἐλαῶν}} to run beyond **the olives**, which stood at the end of the Athenian racecourse, i.e. to go too far, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 995}}, ubi see Scholia; of the Indian **Olea cuspidata**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.4.11.}}
**2** variety of δάφνη {{Refs 1st c.BC+|111, [prev. work] 4.7.2, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.3.6.}}
**II** **olive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 550, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.262, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.119, etc.}} —Acc. to Grammars ἐλάα was the proper form in this sense, ἐλαία in the first; but ἐλάα is simply the _Attic dialect_ form,{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|compare “IG” 12.94.33, 2.476.21, 1055.36 (also “PHal.” 1.98 (3rd c.BC), etc.).}}
**III** **naevus on the skin**, Melamp.{{Refs|p.508F.}}
**IV** ={δίφρου Κυρηναϊκοῦ μέρος}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus [In ἐλάα}}, the _penultimate_ is long, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 360.46, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 550, “Pax” 578, “Av.” 617, etc.}}; but ἐλᾰῶν in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 261.3 (where perhaps ἐλῶν—ἐλᾶς is acknowledged by 2nd c.AD: Aelius Dionysius Grammaticus “fragment” 162, and found in “PRyl.” 97.7 (2nd c.AD), ἐλᾶν in 130.11 (1st c.AD)—should be restored), and ᾰ in ἐλάη, “Anthologia Graeca” 4.2.12 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus), 6.102 ([prev. author]).]}}

1637:
** ἔλαιον**, τό,
(ἐλαία) **olive-oil**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} mostly **anointing-oil**, used after the bath, λοεσσαμένω καὶ ἀλειψαμένω λίπ᾽ ἐλαίῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.577, compare 14.171, 18.350, etc.}}; before wrestling and other gymnastic exercises, πωλησεῦντι τὸ ἔ. εἰς τὸ γυμνάσιον {{Refs|“IG” 12(1).3 (from Rhodes)}}; ἔ. θεῖναι to provide **oil** at the baths, {{Refs|[prev. work]4.597,606 (from Argos): proverbial, πῦρ ἐλαίῳ κοιμίσαι “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 8}} (**a**); ἐλαίῳ πῦρ κατας βεννύναι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 44}}; εὐῶδες ἔ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.339}}; ῥοδόεν (rose-scented) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.186; ἔ. ῥόδινον 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.135}}; ἔ. λευκον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 136; τοῦ λευκοτάτου πάντων ἐ. Σαμιακοῦ 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 331.}}
**II** **any oily substance**, ἔ. χήνειον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.194}}; κίκινον, ἀμυγδάλινον ἔ., etc., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.32,33, etc.; ῥαφάνινον ἔλαιον “PAmh.” 2.93 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; ἔ. ἀπὸ σελαχῶν, like our 'cod-liver **oil**', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 520a18}}; ἔ. ἀπὸ γάλακτος **butter**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 154 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**III** at Athens, **oil-market**, ἀναμενῶ σε.. πρὸς τοὔλαιον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 896.}}

1638:
** ἐλαι-ών**, ῶνος, ὁ,
**olive-yard,** {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 57.2 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Exo.23.11, +others, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.4.14, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.289, “Geoponica” 3.11.1.}}
**II** **the Mount of Olives, Olivet,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Acts.1.12, +others, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 7.9.2.}}

1640:
** ἐλάσσ-ων**,
_Attic dialect_ **ἐλάττων**, ον, _genitive_ ονος: _superlative_ ἐλάχιστος (which see): — **smaller, less**, formed from ἐλαχύς (which see), but serving as _comparative_ to μικρός, δουρηνεκὲς ἢ καὶ ἔλασσον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.357}}; τοὔλασσον ἔχειν to have the **worse, be worse off**, πάντῃ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 269; οὐδὲν ἔλασσον ἔχειν τῇ μάχῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.102; ἔ. ἔχειν παρά τινι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.187; ἐλάττων γίγνεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 441, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.29; οὐκ ἐλάσσονα πάσχειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 813}}; ἐλάττω νομίσας τὴν ἀρχὴν ἢ κατὰ τὴν αὑτοῦ φύσιν εἶναι **too small** for.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.11}}: _absolutely_, **too small**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 23.9}}; **below the average in height,** {{Refs|PLips. 1.9, etc.}}
**2** with _genitive_ _person_, **worse than, inferior to**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 599, etc.}}: but with _genitive_ _of things_, **giving way to, subservient to**, σιτίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 5.8; πάθους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 34}}: _absolutely_, **worse, inferior**, τόποι {{Refs|“Geoponica” 2.48.1.}}
**3** _neuter_ with Preps., περὶ ἐλάσσονος ποιεῖσθαι to consider of **less account**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.6; ἐν ἐλάττονι θέσθαι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.6.12; παρ᾽ ἔλαττον τοῦ δέοντος ἡγεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 546d}}; ἐπ᾽ ἔλαττον (i.e. ἁρμοσθῆναι) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 93b}}; δι᾽ ἐλάσσονος at **less distance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.4; πάντ᾽ ἐν ἐλάττονι ποιεῖσθαι τῆς ἡδονῆς 4th c.BC: Heraclides Ponticus Historicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.537c.}}
**II** of Number, **fewer**, οἱ ἐλάσσονες the **minority**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.121; ἐλάσσονες ἀριθμόν [prev. author] 8.66; ἔ. πλῆθος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.49.}}
**III** of Time, **shorter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 295c, etc.}}
**IV** of worth or rank, οἱ ἐ. the **meaner sort**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.13, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 116.12.}}
**V** _neuter_ ἔλασσον, as _adverb_, ἔ. ἢ μηδέν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 938, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 598, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 564d, etc.}}; ἔ. ἄπωθεν **less** far off, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.67}}; πλείω ἔλαττον, with numbers, more or **less,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|PLips. 28.10 (4th c.AD), etc.: _neuter_ _plural_, as}} _adverb_,= ἐλαττονάκις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 53a, +others: regular}} _adverb_ ἐλασσόνως (which see).
**VI** with _indeclinable_ Numerals, the ἤ of Comparison is often omitted, οὐκ ἐλάττους ὀγδοήκοντα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.8}}; especially in _adverb_ ἔλασσον, as μὴ ἔ. δέκα ἔτη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 856d, +others (Orig. ἐλάχγων, compare ἐλάχ-ιστος, ἐλαχύς.)}}

1641:
** ἐλαττον-έω**,
**receive less**, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.16.18}}; but, **give less**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work = 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica”] 30.15.}}
**2** **waste, be consumed**,{{LXX+4th c.BC+|[prev. work = 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica”] LXX.1Ki.17.16}}; **to be missing, defective,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PMagd. 26.12 (3rd c.AD).}}
**3** _middle_, **lack, want**, {{Refs|[prev. work]11.22.}}

1642:
** ἐλασσ-όω**,
_Attic dialect_ **ἐλαττόω**: _aorist_ ἠλάττωσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.9, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.21.5}}: _perfect_ ἠλλάττωκα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 6, etc.}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-ωθήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.34, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.66}}: _future_ _middle_ in same sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.11, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.104:}}_aorist_ ἠλασσώθην, -ττώθην, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.77, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.33}}: _perfect_ ἠλάττωμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Apollodorus Comicus 7.3, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.4.3}}:—**make less** or **smaller, diminish, reduce in amount,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 19.11 (2nd c.BC), “PLips.” 105.28 (1st c.AD)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 918xi3 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** in early writers, **lower, degrade**, τὴν πόλιν {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.17; ἠλάττωσας αὐτὸν βραχύ τι παρ᾽ ἀγγέλους LXX.Psa.8.6}}; **cut down, shorten**, συναλοιφαῖς τὰ ῥήματα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 6}}: with _genitive_, **detract from**, μὴ προστιθέναι τιμήν, ἀλλὰ μὴ ἐλασσοῦν τῆς ὑπαρχούσης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.42}}:—_middle_, **reduce the power of**, τινάς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 22.15.1.}}
**II** _passive_,
**II.1** _absolutely_, **to be lessened, suffer loss, be depreciated**, of things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.62}}; of persons, {{NT+4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.59, +others, “OGI” 139.10(2nd c.BC), “PTeb.” 382.13 (1st c.BC), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.32 O., +others, NT.John.3.30, etc.; μέγα τοῦθ᾽ οἱ πατέρες ἠλαττώμεθα 4th-3rd c.BC: Apollodorus Comicus 7.3}}; also, **take less than one's due, waive one's rights** or **privileges**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.14}}; but, **fall short of one's professions, act dishonestly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.49.}}
**II.2** with _dative_ _of things_, **have the worst of it**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.11, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.104, etc.; τῷ πολέμῳ [prev. author] 1.115}}; **to be inferior**, τῇ ἐμπειρίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.72; πολλαῖς ναυσί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.15; πᾶσι τούτοις [prev. work] 6.2.28}}; ἠλαττωμένος τοῖς ὄμμασι, of a one-eyed man, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.4.3; πρός τινα “POxy.” 215ii18}}: with _genitive_, **fall short of**, τῶν ἀρχετύπων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.606.}}
**II.3** with _genitive_ _person_, **to be at a disadvantage with** a person, πολλὰ μὲν οὖν ἔγωγ᾽ ἐλαττοῦμαι κατὰ τουτονὶ τὸν ἀγῶν᾽ Αἰσχίνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.3; ἐλαττοῦσθαί τινός τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.121b; μηδὲν τῶν δημιουργῶν [prev. author] “Grg.” 459c.}}
**II.4** with _genitive_ _of things_, **suffer loss in respect of**, κεφαλαίου, τόκων, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 155.10 (2nd c.AD)}}; **to be in want of**, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.21.15(16): also with _dative_, LXX.2Ki.3.29.}}

1643:
** ἐλαύνω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.62, etc.: _Ionic dialect_}}_imperfect_ ἐλαύνεσκον (ἀπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.119:}}_future_ ἐλάσω [ᾰ], _participle_ ἐλάσοντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.55 codices, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.36, (ἐξ-) 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τόπων τῶν κατὰ ἄνθρωπον” 46, “Nat.Mul.” 32 (ἐλάσσω}} (παρ-) is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.427, and ξυνελάσσομεν}} is _subjunctive_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.39); ἐλάω 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.411}}; _Attic dialect_ ἐλῶ, ᾷς, ᾷ, _infinitive_ ἐλᾶν, also {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.207, etc., and so 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} in the resolved form ἐλόω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.315, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.319}}: _infinitive_ ἐλάαν (though this is also _infinitive_ _present_, see below) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.496, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.290}}: _aorist 1_ ἤλᾰσα, _Epic dialect_ ἔλᾰσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.80, ἔλασσα 18.564}}, _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐλάσασκεν {{Refs|2.199:}}_perfect_ ἐλήλᾰκα (ἀπ-, ἐξ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 828:}}_pluperfect_ ἐληλάκειν (ἐξ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.90}}:— _middle_ (see. below {{Refs|1.2)}}, _future_ ἐλάσομαι (παρ-) uncertain reading in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 3.30.3}}: _aorist_ ἠλασάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.682}}, rare in _Attic dialect_, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 484b}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἤλσατο {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Ibycus Lyricus 55}}; _Epic dialect_ ἐλάσαιο, -ασαίατο, -ασσάμενος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.51, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.537, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.637:}} —_passive_, _future_ ἐλασθήσομαι (ἐξ-) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.9:}}_aorist_ ἠλάθην [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 430, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 4; later ἠλάσθην “Anthologia Graeca” 7.278 (1st c.BC: Archias), “Sammelb.” 997 (4th c.AD), (ἐξ-, συν-) 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.24.9, 18.22.6, etc. (in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} the Mss. vary between the two forms, ἐξελαθείς {{Refs|7.165, ἀπηλάσθησαν 3.54)}}: _perfect_ ἐλήλαμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.113, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.84}} (ἐξ-), etc.; ἐλήλασμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.133, 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 31.4 (probably), (ἐξ-) 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.22.4, (συν-) 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 233.30}}: _pluperfect_ ἠλήλατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.400; poetry also ἐλήλατο 4.135}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἠλήλαντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 143, also ἐληλέδατ᾽, ἐληλέατ᾽, ἐληλάδατ᾽ variants in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.86.}}— The _present_ **ἐλάω** is rare and mainly poetical, _imperative_ ἔλα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).38, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 332, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 819, “fragment” 779.1}} (also non-thematic _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐλάντω {{Refs|“SIG” 1025.8 (from Cos))}}: _infinitive_ ἐλᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cantharus Comicus 4, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.32:}}_infinitive_ ἐλάαν as _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ _present_ is frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below 1.2)}}: _participle_ ἐλάουσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 4.5}}: _imperfect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔλων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.2}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔλαεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.872; ἀπ-έλα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.32}}; but ἀπ-ήλαον in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1001 is probably an error for -ήλα᾽αν, _Doric dialect_ for -ήλασαν}}:—_radical_ sense, **drive, set in motion**, of driving flocks, εἰς εὐρὺ σπέος ἤλασε μῆλα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.237; κακοὺς δ᾽ ἐς μέσσον ἔλασσεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.299}}_aorist_ _middle_ ἠλασάμην in _active_ sense, {{Refs|10.537, 11.682}}: frequently of horses, chariots, ships, **drive**, ἐλάαν (_infinitive_ _present_) ἅρμα καὶ ἵππους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.334; ἐς τὴν ἀγορὴν τὸ ζεῦγος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59}}; ἐ. ἵππον **ride** it, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.64, +others}}; κέλητας καὶ ἅρματα ἐ. **ride** and **drive**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.86}}; ἐ. νῆα **row** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.109, etc.}}; στρατὸν ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).66, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.176, 4.91, etc.}}
**b** with _accusative_ omitted, _intransitive_, **go in a chariot, drive**, μάστιξεν δ᾽ ἐλάαν (i.e. ἵππους) he whipped them **on**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.366, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 734, 739}}; βῆ δ᾽ ἐλάαν ἐπὶ κύματα he **drove on** over the waves, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.27}}; διὰ νύκτα ἐλάαν **travel** the night through, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.50}}; ἐς τὸ ἄστυ ἐ. **drive** into the city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60; ἐπὶ ζευγέων ἐ. [prev. work] 199}}; **ride**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.88, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 3.9, etc.}}; ἐλῶν ἐς Θρηΐκην **marching**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.89, etc.}}; **row**, μάλα σφοδρῶς ἐλάαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.124}}; ἐλαύνοντες **rowers**, {{Refs|13.22, etc.}}
**c** in this _intransitive_ sense, it sometimes took an _accusative_ loci, γαλήνην ἐλαύνειν **to sail** the calm sea, i.e. over it, {{Refs|7.319}}; so τὰ ἕσπερα νῶτ᾽ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 731 (Lyric poetry)}}; also ἐλαύνειν δρόμον **run** a course, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 28; ὁδόν 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 586.}}
**d** _passive_, [νηῦς] ἐλαυνομένη a ship **under way**, {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.155 (but πλοῖα ὑπὸ σκληρῶν ἀνέμων ἐλαυνόμενα NT.Jam.3.4)}}; τὰ κατάντη ἐλαύνεσθαι, of horses, **to be ridden** on steep ground, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 8.3.}}
**2** **drive away, carry off**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} of stolen cattle or horses, βοῶν ἀρίστας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.353; ἵππους 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.236; ἐ. ὅ τι δύναιντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.18}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.637, 20.51; ῥύσι᾽ ἐλαυνόμενος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.674, etc.}}
**3** **drive away, expel**, ἐ. [τινὰ] ἐκ δήμου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|6.158; ἄνδρας ἀπ᾽ Οἰνώνας 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.16: frequently in Trag., ἐ. τινὰ γῆς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 70}}; μύσος, μίασμα ἐ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 967 codices, “Eu.” 283 (_passive_),compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 98}}; ἄγος ἐ.,= ἀγηλατέω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126; ἐ. λῃστάς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1188, etc.}}:—_passive_, γῆν πρὸ γῆς ἐλαύνομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 682.}}
**4** **drive** (to extremities), **persecute, plague**, οἵ μιν ἄδην ἐλόωσι.. πολέμοιο who will **harass** him till he has had enough of war, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.315}}; ἔτι μέν μίν φημι ἄδην λάαν κακότητος I think I **shall persecute** him till he has had enough, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.290; θεὸς ἐλαύνει πόλιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 28; Ἰωνίαν ἤλασεν βίᾳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 771; μή τι δαιμόνιον τὰ πράγματα ἐλαύνῃ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.54; σὺ δ᾽ ἀπειλεῖς πᾶσιν, ἐλαύνεις πάντας [prev. author] 21.135, compare 173}}:—_passive_, ἐλαυνομένων καὶ ὑβριζομένων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 18.48; λύπῃ πᾶς ἐλήλαται κακῇ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 275; κακοῖς πρός τινος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 31; ὑπ᾽ ἀνάγκης καὶ οἴστρου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 240d; τὴν ψυχὴν ἐρωτικῇ μανίᾳ 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 14.18}}; ἐλαύνεσθαι τὴν γνώμην **to be out of one's** mind, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.27.5.}}
**5** ={βινέω}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 39, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Comedy texts 3.4.}}
**6** _intransitive_ in expressions like{ἐς τοσοῦτον ἤλασαν} they **drove it** so far (where πρᾶγμα must be supplied), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.50; ἐς πᾶσαν κακότητα [prev. author] 2.124}}; εἰς κόρον ἐλαύνειν **push matters** till disgust ensued, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 11.10}}; εἰς ἴσον (i.e. τισί) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus “Praef.” 4}}: hence, **push on, go on**, ἐγγὺς μανιῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 904 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἔξω τοῦ φρονεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author]}}***ba.**{{Refs|853}}; πόρρω ἐ. σοφίας **go** far in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 4b, compare “Grg.” 486a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.39.}}
**II** **strike**, ἐλάτῃσιν πόντον ἐλαύνοντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.6; κιθάραν πλήκτρῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 351 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** **strike** with a weapon, but never with a missile, τὸν σκήπτρῳ ἐλάσασκεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.199; ξίφει ἤλασε κόρσην 5.584; κόρυθος φάλον ἤλασεν 13.614}}; ὀδόντας ἐ. **knock out**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.785: with double _accusative_, τὸν μὲν.. μεταδρομάδην ἔλασ᾽ ὦμον}} him he **struck** on.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.80}}; χθόνα δ᾽ ἤλασε παντὶ μετώπῳ **struck** earth with his forehead, of a falling man, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.94}}: with _accusative_ _cognate_, **inflict** a wound, οὐλὴν τήν ποτέ με σῦς ἤλασε {{Refs|21.219}}:—_passive_, with _accusative_ νῶτον ὄπισθ᾽ αἰχμῇ δουρὸς ἐληλαμένος {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 11.20; ἐλαύνεται εἰς τὸν μηρόν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 61.}}
**II.3** **strike** one thing against another, πρὸς γῆν ἐ. κάρη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.237}}; of weapons, **drive through**, διαπρὸ χαλκὸν ἔλασσε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|22.295; [δόρυ] διὰ στήθεσφιν ἔλασσε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.57, compare 20.269; ἤλασε Λυγκέος ἐν πλευραῖσι χαλκόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.70}}:—_passive_, **go through**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.135, 13.595}}; **to be fixed in**, ὀϊστὸς ὤμῳ ἐνὶ στιβαρῷ ἠλήλατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5.400; διὰ [σφονδύλου] διαμπερὲς ἐληλάσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 616e.}}
**III** _metaphorically_,
**III.1** **beat out** metal, **forge**, ἀσπίδα.. ἣν ἄρα χαλκεὺς ἤλασεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.296}}; πέντε πτύχας ἤλασε **beat out** five plates, {{Refs|20.270}}; περὶ δ᾽ ἕρκος ἔλασσε κασσιτέρου **make** a fence **of beaten** tin (with a play on _signification_ {{Refs|2), 18.564}}; εὐνὴ Ἡφαίστου χερσὶν ἐληλαμένη χρυσοῦ a bed of **beaten** gold, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 12.6}}; σίδηρος λεπτῶς ἐληλ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 41.}}
**III.2** **draw a line of** wall, trench, etc., ἀμφὶ δὲ τάφρον ἤλασαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.450; ἀμφὶ δὲ τεῖχος ἔλασσε πόλει 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.9; σταυροὺς δ᾽ ἐκτὸς ἔλασσε 14.11; τοῖχοι ἐληλέατ᾽ 7.86}}; τεῖχος τοὺς ἀγκῶνας ἐς τὸν ποταμὸν ἐλήλαται the wall **has its** angles **carried down** to the river, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.180, compare 185, 191}}; ἐληλαμέναι πέρι πύργον **having** a wall **built** round, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 872 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὄγμον ἐλαύνειν **work one's way** down a ridge or swathe in reaping or mowing, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.68; ἐ. αὔλακα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 443}}; ἀμπελίδος ὄρχον ἐ. **to draw** a line of vines, i.e. **plant** them **in line**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 995}}: generally, **plant, produce**, ἐλᾷ τέσσαρας ἀρετὰς αἰών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.74.}}
**III.3** κολῳὸν ἐλαύνειν **prolong, keep up** the brawl, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.575.}}
**III.4** ἐξ ὄσσων ἐς γαῖαν ἐ. δάκρυ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 96.}}

1644:
** ἐλαφρ-ία**, ἡ,
**lightness**: **levity**, {{NT|NT.2Cor.1.17.}}
**II** **alleviation**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 2.2.}}
**III** ={ὀλιγότης}, {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}

1645:
** ἐλαφρ-ός**, ά, όν,
and in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.20}} ός, όν: (see. ἐλαχύς);— **light in weight**, τόν οἱ ἐ. ἔθηκε (i.e. λᾶαν) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.450; ξύλου ἐλαφρότερα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.23; πῦρ 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.57}}; opposed to βαρύς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 63c, etc.}}; in Epitaphs, γαῖαν ἔχοις ἐλαφράν '**sit tibi terra levis',** {{Refs|epigram Gr. 195 (from Vaxos), compare “Sammelb.” 315.}} _adverb_, τά (i.e. δένδρεα){{Refs 8th c.BC+|οἱ πλώοιεν ἐλαφρῶς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.240.}}
**2** **light to bear, easy**, καί κεν ἐλαφρότερος πόλεμος Τρώεσσι γένοιτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.287; συμφ ορὰν ἐλαφροτέραν καταστῆσαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.12; πόνος -ότερος ἑαυτοῦ συνηθείῃ γίνεται 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 241}}: later, _comparative_ ἐλαφρώτερον ἄλγος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC(?): Maximus Astrologus 173}}; ἐλαφρόν [ἐστι] 'tis **light, easy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.77, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 265, etc.}}; **easy to understand**, [προβλήματα] ἐ. καὶ πιθανά {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.133e, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 18.11}}; ἐν ἐλαφρῷ ποιήσασθαί τι to make **light** of a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.154; οὐκ ἐν ἐ. ποιεῖσθαι [prev. author] 1.118}}; οὐκ ἐν ἐ. no **light matter**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.212.}} _adverb_ -ρῶς, φέρειν ζυγόν to bear it **lightly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.93.}}
**3** **light of digestion**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.137a.}}
**4** **shallow**, διάπλους {{Refs|“Periplus Maris Rubri [1st c.AD]” 55; δῖναι [prev. work]40.}}
**5** _active_, **ease-giving**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 8, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.92.}}
**II** **light in moving, nimble**, γυῖα δ᾽ ἔθηκεν ἐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.122; ἦ μάλ᾽ ἐ. ἀνήρ 16.745; ἐλαφρότατος ποσσί 23.749}}; χεῖρες.. ἐπαΐσσονται ἐ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|. [prev. work] 628; κίρκος.. ἐλαφρότατος πετεηνῶν 22.139, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.87; [ἵπποι] ἐλαφρότατοι θείειν 3.370; ἐλαφραῖς πτερύγων ῥιπαῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 125 (_anapaest meter_); ἐ. ποδί [prev. work] 281 (_anapaest meter_)}}; γονάτων ἐλαφρὸν ὁρμάν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.20; ἐ. ποδῶν ἴχνι᾽ ἀειράμεναι Callias Epigrammaticus “anonymous fragment” 391; ἐλαφρὰ ἡλικία}} the age of **active** youth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.27}}; ἐλαφροί, οἱ, **light troops**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 4.2.27}} (restricted to cavalry who fight at close quarters, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 1.3): _metaphorically_, πόλιας θῆκεν ἐλαφροτέρας}} made them **easier in condition,** {{Refs|epigram Gr. 905 (from Gortyn).}} _adverb_ -ρῶς **nimbly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 217; ὀρχεῖσθαι πυρρίχην 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.12.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **light-minded, unsteady, fickle**, πᾶν πλῆθός ἐστιν ἐ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.56.11}}; ἐ. λύσσα **light-headed** madness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 851.}}
**III.b** **gentle, mild**, σφᾶς αὐτοὺς -οτάτους τοῖς συνοῦσι παρέχοντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.31, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 360c.}}
**III.2** **small**, ποταμός {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.17.7}}; **of small power** or **strength**, πόλεις {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.62.6.}}
**III.3** **relieved** of a burden, ψυχὴ ἐ. καὶ δι᾽ αὑτῆς {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.3.32.}}
**IV** Ἐλαφρός· Ζεὺς ἐν Κρήτῃ, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

1646:
** ἐλᾰχιστ-ος** [ᾰ], η, ον,
_superlative_ of ἐλαχύς: _comparative_ ἐλάσσων (which see):— **smallest, least**, frequently with a _negative_, γέρας, δύναμις οὐκ ἐ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 573, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.168, etc.}}; λόγου ἐλαχίστου of **least** account, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.143}}; ἐλαχίστου ἐδέησε διαφθεῖραι **narrowly** missed destroying them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.77; περὶ ἐλαχίστου ποιεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30a; παρ᾽ ἐλάχιστον ἐποίησεν αὐτοὺς ἀφαιρεθῆναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 17.22.}}
**2** of Time, **shortest**, δι᾽ ἐλαχίστου (i.e. χρόνου) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39}}; δι᾽ ἐλαχίστης βουλῆς with **shortest** deliberation, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.138.}}
**3** of Number, **fewest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 378a; ἐ. τὸν ἀριθμόν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1312a30}}; ἐν ἐλαχίστοις δυσίν between two **at least.** {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1131a15.}}
**4** in Mathematics texts, ἐλάχιστα καὶ μέγιστα **minima** and maxima, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 1 “Praef.”}}
**II** τὸ ἐλάχιστον, τοὐλάχιστον, **at the least**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.8, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.21}}; ἐλάχιστα **least** of any one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70}}; ὡς ἐ. as **little as possible**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 63d.}}
**III** from ἐλάχιστος came a new _comparative_ ἐλαχιστότερος **less than the least**, ἐ. πάντων ἁγίων {{NT|NT.Eph.3.8: _superlative_ ἐλαχιστότατος}} **very least of all**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 3.54, 9.406.}}

