1657:
** ἐλευθ-ερία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **freedom, liberty**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.61, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.62, 95; ἐλευθερίας φῶς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 809 (Lyric poetry), compare 863 (_anapaest meter_)}}; δι᾽ ἐλευθερίας μόλις ἐξῆλθες, i.e. μόλις ἠλευθερώθης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1509 (_anapaest meter_); ὑπῆρξαν ἐλευθερίας τῆ Ἑλλάδι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.142}}; **freedom from** a thing, ἀπὸ πασῶν ἀρχῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 698a; τινός [prev. author] “R.” 329c, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 6.228 (from Adaeus).}}
**b** **manumission**, ἡ εἰκοστὴ τῶν ἐ.,= Latin _vicesima manumissionum_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 326ii 11 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** **licence**, ἀκολασία καὶ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 492c}}; of Diogenes, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.185c.}}
**3** later, ={ἐλευθεριότης}, {{Refs|“UPZ” 62.7.}}
**4** name of a **dance**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.293.}}

1658:
** ἐλεύθερος**, α, ον
(ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 328, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 868)}}: later **ἐλαύθερος** {{Refs|“BCH” 22.76(Delph.)}}; Elean **ἐλεύθαρος** {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 416.3}}:— **free**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} has the word only in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” in two phrases, ἐλεύθερον ἦμαρ}} the day **of freedom**, i.e.**freedom**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.455, 16.831, +others}}; and κρητὴρ ἐλεύθερος the cup drunk **to freedom**,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|6.528; ἐ. πιοῦσαν οἶνον ἀποθανεῖν 4th c.BC: Xenarchus Comicus 5 codices 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus (perhaps -ριον,compare ἐλευθέριος 1.2)}}; of persons, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 25.11, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.6, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 50, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1020, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.15, etc.}}: _comparative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.21}}: _superlative_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hier.” 1.16}}; τὸ ἐ. **freedom**, Hdt {{Refs|7.103}}, etc.; τοὐλεύθερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 438}}: c._genitive_, **free** or **freed from** a thing, φόνου, πημάτων, φόβου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 603 codices, “Ch.” 1060, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 869; αἰτίας 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 272}}; ἔξω αἰτίας ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 445}}; ἐ. ἀπ᾽ ἀλλήλων **independent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.23, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 832d.}}
**b** ἐλευθέρα, ἡ, **married woman**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.571d}}; **wife,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|POxy. 1872.8(5th-6th c.AD)}}; but, **freedwoman,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.2490 (from Vienne).}}
**c** **free**, of cities, in Roman Law, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“BGU” 316.3 (4th c.AD).}}
**2** of things, **free, open to all**, ἀγορά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.3}}; ἐ. φυλακή,= Latin _libera custodia_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.46; περιωπή 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 15.5}}; **unencumbered**, of property, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.21, “IG” 9(1).32.10 (from Stiris), “SIG” 364.36 (Ephesus, 3rd c.BC).}}
**3** ἐλεύθερον εἶναί τινι, with _infinitive_, **legally permissible, open to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]45.42 (Halic., 5th c.BC).}}
**II** ={ἐλευθέριος}, **fit for a freeman, free, frank**, φρήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.57; ἐλευθερωτέρη ὑπόκρισις 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116; ἐλεύθρα βάζειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 593 (Lyric poetry); ὦ μηδὲν ὑγιὲς μηδ᾽ ἐ. φρονῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1006; δούλη μέν, εἴρηκεν δ᾽ ἐ. λόγον [prev. author] “Tr.” 63, compare “El.” 1256; φρονήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 567a}}; βάσανοι ἐ. tortures **such as might be used to a freeman**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 946c (so φάσγανα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 495.38)}}; τὸ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 245c}}: frequently in _adverb_ -ρως, εἰπεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.93, +others}}; χαίρειν.. καὶ γελᾶν ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1300; τεθραμμένους 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.43 codices (perhaps -ερίως)}}; παιδευθεὶς ἐ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.154 codices (perhaps -ερίως); ἐ. δούλευε, δοῦλος οὐκ ἔσει 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 857}}; ἐλεύθεροι ἐλευθέρως free and **like free men**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 919e}}. (Cf. Latin _līber_, from Ital. ***loufero**-(cf. Osc. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Luvfreis” 'Liberi'), I.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} (**e)leudh-ero**-: the connexion with Slavonic _liud_, OHG. **liut**, etc. 'people' is doubtful.)

1659:
** ἐλευθερ-όω**,
**set free**, τὰς Ἀθήνας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.62; Ἰωνίην [prev. author] 4.137; πατρίδα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 403; πόλιν [prev. author] “Ch.” 1046, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.144; δούλους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.15, etc.}}; ἐ. τὸν ἔσπλουν **set** the entrance **free, clear** it, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.51}}; **release** a debtor, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.59}}; τό γ᾽ εἰς ἑαυτὸν πᾶν ἐλευθεροῖ στόμα he **keeps** his tongue altogether **free**, i.e. does not commit himself by speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 706}}; **free from blame, acquit**, τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.26}}:—_passive_, **to be set free**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95, 127, +others; τυράννων [prev. author] 5.62}}; **indulge in licence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 575a.}}
**2** c._genitive_, **set free, release from**, φόνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1449; χρεῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 566e; ἀρότρου βοῦν 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 5.23}}; also ἐλευθεροῦντες ἐκ δρασμῶν πόδα, i.e. ceasing to flee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1010:}} —_passive_, τῶνδε τῶν τόπων ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 114b; ἀπὸ τῶν πλουσίων [prev. author] “R.” 569a.}}

1660:
** ἔλευσις**, εως, ἡ,
**coming, arrival**, εἰς βίον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 28, compare 12th c.AD: Tzetzes, Joannes Grammaticus “Historiarum variarum chiliades” 7.572, Scholia 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.887, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**2** **the Advent** of Christ, {{NT|NT.Act.7.52.}}

1661:
** ἐλεφάντ-ῐνος**, η, ον,
**of ivory**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 33.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1169, “Pl.” 815, +others}}; δίφρος ἐ.,= Latin _sella curulis_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.53.9 (_plural_), +others}}; οἶκοι ἐ. {{LXX|LXX.Amos.3.15}}; τὸ ἐ. the **substance of ivory**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 290c.}}
**2** **white as ivory**, μέτωπον {{Refs|“Anacreontea” 15.12}}; τάριχος Crates 29.

1667:
** ἑλίσσω**
or **ἐλίσσω** (the latter more frequently in codices of {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}, _Attic dialect_ **ἑλίττω**, _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ **-έμεν**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.309}}; _Ionic dialect_ **εἰλίσσω** or **εἱλίσσω** (εἱ. is found in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below), but κατ-ελίσσειν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.(Sp.”) 37, κατειλίξαι [prev. author] “Morb.” 2.18, +others)}}: _future_ ἑλίξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 711}}: _aorist_ εἴλιξα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 73a (εἵλ-}} codices, but κατ-ειλίξας {{Refs|“IG” 22.204.32)}}; _participle_ ἑλίξας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.466}}, _Ionic dialect_ εἰλίξας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.34}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.320}}: _future_ ἑλίξομαι {{Refs|17.728}}: _aorist_ ἑλιξάμην {{Refs|12.467, 17.283}}:—_passive_,_future_ ἑλιγήσομαι {{LXX|LXX.Isa.34.4}}: _aorist 1_ εἱλίχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 358}}; _participle_ ἑλιχθείς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.74}}: _perfect_ εἵλιγμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 791, ἐλήλιγμαι 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.17.12}}: _pluperfect_ εἵλικτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 927}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἱλίχατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.90.}} —The _Ionic dialect_ form is found in Trag. (see. below, codices usually εἱλ-; but τ᾽ εἰ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 138 (Lyric poetry, codex _middle_),compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1314, 1348 (codex Rav.)), in “IG” [same place], and codices of 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus (as “Ti.” [same place], ἀν-ειλίττων “Phlb.” 15e)}}; ἐπειλίξας is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.161. (ϝελ-, ἐϝελ-, compare εἴλω, ἐλελίζω ad near the end)}}:—**turn round** or **about**: _active_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always of **turning** a chariot **round the doublingpost**, οἶσθα γὰρ εὖ περὶ τέρματ᾽ ἐλισσέμεν [ἵππους] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.309,compare 466.}}
**2** generally, **roll**, ἑ. βίου πόρον **roll** life's stream **along**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).15}}; of the chariot of Day, αἰθὴρ κοινὸν φάος εἱλίσσων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1092 (_anapaest meter_); ἥλιος.. εἱλίσσων φλόγα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 3}}; εἰ. κόνιν **roll the eddying** dust, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1085 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ἑ. δίνας, of the Euripus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 7, compare 1103 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἑ. κόρας, βλέφαρα, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HF” 868 (troch.), “Or.” 1266 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** of any rapid motion, ἅλιον.. ἑ. πλάταν **ply** it **swiflly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 358 (Lyric poetry)}}; of the dance, ἑ. πόδα **move the swift** foot, conjecture in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 171 (Lyric poetry), compare “IA” 215 (Lyric poetry)}}; εἱ. θιάσους **lead the dancing** bands, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “IT” 1145 (Lyric poetry); ἑ. χορούς 5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 66.5}}: _absolutely_, **dance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 234 (Lyric poetry), compare “Or.” 1292 (whence ἑ. τινά}} **dance in honour of**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “HF” 690 (Lyric poetry), “IA” 1480 (Lyric poetry))}}; ἑ. βωμόν **dance round** it, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 321.}}
**4** **roll** or **wind round**, πλόκαμον περὶ ἄτρακτον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.34, compare 2.38}}; λίνον ἠλακάτᾳ δακτύλοις ἑ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1432 (Lyric poetry)}}; χεῖρας ἀμφὶ γόνυ ἑ. **clasp** them round.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1622.}}
**5** _metaphorically_, **turn in one's mind, revolve**, τοιαῦθ᾽ ἑ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 231, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 978d; μῆτιν 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.463}}; ἑ. κακοὺς λόγους **speak wily** words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 892.}}
**6** κόλπους ἑ. **form winding** reaches, of rivers, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 630; ἀγκῶνας [prev. author] 979.}}
**II** _middle_ and _passive_, **turn oneself round** or **about** (but in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.49 εἱλίσσεθ᾽ ἑταίρους (as read by Nicanor)}} **rallied his** comrades), ἑλιχθέντων ὑπ᾽ Ἀχαιῶν **when** they **turned to face** the foe, {{Refs|[prev. work] 74, compare 408}}; so of a wild boar, ἑλιξάμενος **having turned to bay**, {{Refs|17.283}}; of a serpent, **coil himself**, ἑλισσόμενος περὶ χειῇ {{Refs|22.95}}; ἡ δέ τ᾽ ἐλισσομένη πέτεται (i.e. καλαῦροψ) the shepherd's staff flies **spinning through the air**, {{Refs|23.846}}; κνίση.. ἑλισσομένη περὶ καπνῷ **rolling** with the smoke, {{Refs|1.317}}; ἑλισσόμενοι περὶ δίνας **whirled round** in the eddies, {{Refs|21.11}}; of a river, δίνῃς ἀργυρέῃς εἱλιγμένος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 791, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.32}}; of the waves, τὸ ἑλισσόμενον αἰεὶ κυμάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.55}}; of ocean, ἑλίσσεσθαι περὶ πᾶσαν χθόνα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 138}}; ὧραι ἑλισσόμεναι the **circling** hours, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.3.}}
**II.2** **turn hither and thither, go about**, ἀν᾽ ὅμιλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.49; καθ᾽ ὅμιλον [prev. work] 467}}; ἑλίσσετο ἔνθα καὶ ἔνθα **turned himself** hither and thither, doubting what to do, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.24.}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, **to be constantly in** or **about** a thing, περὶ φύσας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.372}}; ἔν τινι, εἴς τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 194b, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus cited in 4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 3.4: with _genitive_, μέλιτός τε καὶ ἔργων εἱλίσσονται (i.e. μέλισσαι) 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 1030.}}
**II.4** **whirl in the dance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 569 (Lyric poetry), “IA” 1055 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.5** _middle_ in _active_ sense, ἧκε δέ μιν σφαιρηδὸν ἑλιξάμενος he threw it **with a whirl** like a ball, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.204.}}
**II.6** τὰς κεφαλὰς εἱλίχατο μίτρῃσι **have their** heads **rolled round** with turbans, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.90.}}

1668:
** ἕλκος**, εος, τό,
**wound**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.190, +others (never in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.91, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1232 (_plural_), etc.}}
**2** **festering wound, sore, ulcer**, ἕ. ὕδρου **the festering bite** of a serpent, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.723}}; **plague-ulcer**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 5.1, etc. (2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.232 defines ἕ. as ἡ τῆς συνεχείας λύσις ἐν σαρκώδει μορίῳ}}, and both 1.1 and 1.2 are treated in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Ulc.”}}; ἕ. is applied to amputations in {{Refs|“Art.” 68.)}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **wound, loss**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 4.17, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 652, +others; ἕ. δήμιον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 640}}; ὑποκάρδιον ἕ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.15; γίγνεται ἕ. ἐφ᾽ ἕλκει 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 1063.6. (Orig. Ελκος}}, cf. Latin _ulcus_, Sanskrit_árśas_ (n.) 'haemorrhoid': ἕ- by influence of ἕλκω.)

1669:
** ἑλκόω**,
**wound, lacerate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 405; ἑ. ὄνυξιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 630a5, etc.}}
**2** **ulcerate**, βλέφαρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 19, +others}}:—_passive_, of persons, **to suffer from wounds** or **sores,** {{NT|Comica Adespota 106.8, NT.Luke.16.20}}; of sores, **suppurate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.5.}}
**3** **make an incision in** a tree, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.16.1 (_passive_), “CP” 3.2.2 (_passive_).}}
**II** _metaphorically_, ἑ. φρένας, οἴκους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 878 (Lyric poetry), “Supp.” 223}}:—_passive_, τὴν διάνοιαν ἑλκοῦσθαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.551.}}

1670:
** ἕλκω**
(ἑλκύω late, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|12th c.AD: Tzetzes, Joannes Grammaticus “Historiarum variarum chiliades” 6.621), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.52, etc.}}, _imperfect_
**A** εἷλκον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 39, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ ἕλκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.213, +others (never εἵλκυον)}}: _future_ ἕλξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 909, etc., rarely ἑλκύσω [ῠ] 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 2, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 174}}: _aorist_ εἵλκῠσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 232, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.103}}, Trag. and _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 987, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 540, “SIG” 2587.23, +others, etc.; ἥλκυσα “IG” 11(2).287 B 61 (Delos, 2nd c.BC), “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4993,5006 (Egypt, 3rd c.AD)}}; later εἷλξα, _poetry_ ἕλξα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.370 (Tiberius Rhetor Ill.), 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 258, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ φυσικῶν δυνάμεων - Scripta Minora” 1.12}}: _perfect_ εἵλκῠκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.59}}; _perfect_ _participle_ ἑολκώς probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 177:}} —_middle_, _future_ -ύσομαι (ἐφ-) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 6.10.9:}}_aorist_ εἱλκυσάμην (ἀφ-) variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 11}}, _subjunctive_ ἀφελκύσωμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1120; rarely εἱλξάμην 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 4.534}}:—_passive_, _future_ ἑλκυσθήσομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 614 (ξυγκαθ-), 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 358, ἑλχθήσομαι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 7.7}}: _aorist_ εἱλκύσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.14, (ἐξ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 688, ἑλκ- 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.140, ἡλκ- “IG” 12(7).115.11 (from Amorgos)}}; later εἵλχθην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.11, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 8.15, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.91}}: _perfect_ εἵλκυσμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἐπικυήσιος” 16, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 576, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.316, (καθ-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.50, ἕλκυσμαι (ἀν-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.98, ἥλκυσμαι “BGU” 1256.11 (2nd c.BC)}}: _pluperfect_ εἵλκυστο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.36.}} —In _Attic dialect_, ἕλκω, ἕλξω were alone used in _present_ and _future_, while the other tenses were formed from ἑλκυ-; compare ἑλκέω (which see), ἑλκυστάζω. In {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus}} rejected the augment (Cf. Latin _sulcus_, Lithuanian _velkù_ 'drag'):— **draw, drag**, with collateralnotion of force or exertion, ὣς εἰπὼν ποδὸς ἕλκε **began to drag** [the dead body] by the foot, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.383; ἤν περ.. ποδῶν ἕλκωσι θύραζε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.276; τινὰ τῆς ῥινός 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 73; Ἕκτορα.. περὶ σῆμ᾽ ἑτάροιο ἕλκει 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.52}}; **drag away** a prisoner, {{Refs|22.65 (_passive_)}}; **draw** ships down to the sea, {{Refs|2.152}}, etc.; **draw along** a felled tree, {{Refs|17.743}}; of mules, **draw** a chariot, {{Refs|24.324}}; ἑλκέμεναι νειοῖο.. πηκτὸν ἄροτρον **draw** the plough **through** the field, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|10.353, compare 23.518; ἕ. τινὰ ἐπὶ κνάφου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.92}}; περιβαλόντας σχοινία ἕ. **haul at** them, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.85.}}
**A.2** **draw after one**, ἐν δ᾽ ἔπεσ᾽ Ὠκεανῷ.. φάος ἠελίοιο, ἕλκον νύκτα μέλαιναν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.486}}; πέδας ἕ. **trail** fetters **after one**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.129}}; ἕ. χλανίδα **let** one's cloak **trail behind**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 19 (_anapaest meter_); θοἰμάτιον 5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 45.}}
**A.3** **tear** **in pieces** (used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in the form ἑλκέω), ὀνύχεσσι παρειάν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 280}}; **worry**, τὰς κύνας ὥλαφος ἕλκοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.135; ἑλκυσθῆναι ὑπὸ κυνός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.140.}}
**A.3.b** _metaphorically_, **carp at**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.103.}}
**A.4** **draw** a bow, ἕλκε.. γλυφίδας τε λαβὼν καὶ νεῦρα βόεια {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.122, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.419, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.21, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.28, etc.}}
**A.5** **draw** a sword, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1233, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 576 (_passive_)}}:—_middle_, ἕλκετο δ᾽ ἐκ κολεοῖο.. ξίφος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.194.}}
**A.6** ἕ. ἱστία **hoist** sails, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.426}}:—also in _middle_,{{Refs|“hymnus ad Bacchum” 32.}}
**A.7** **lift up** scales, so as to poise them, ἕλκε δὲ μέσσα λαβών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.72, 22.212.}}
**A.II** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}
**A.II.1** **pull** a barge-pole, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.194.}}
**A.II.2** **tow** a ship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.90, etc.}}
**A.II.3** **drag into court**, ἕλκω σε κλητεύσοντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1218, compare 1004 (_passive_)}}; εἰς ἀγοράν {{NT|NT.Act.16.19}}; **drag about**, especially with lewd violence, ἕλκει καὶ βιάζεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.150; μηδένα ἕλξειν μηδ᾽ ὑβριεῖν [prev. work] 221; ἕλκειν γυναῖκα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.12: _metaphorically_, ἄνω κάτω τοὺς λόγους ἕ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 195c, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 167a35; ἡμέας ὁ καιρὸς ἕλκει 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.10}}; also ἥλκυσμαι λαμπαδάρχης I **have been compelled to serve** as λ., {{Refs|“BGU” [prev. cited]}}
**A.II.4** **draw** or **suck up**, [ἥλιος] ἕλκει τὸ ὕδωρ ἐπ᾽ ἑωυτόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.25}}; ἕ. τὸν ἀέρα **draw** it **in, breathe** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 19, 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 101d (_passive_),compare 5th c.BC: Philyllius Comicus 20: ζωὴν φύσιν Archel. cited in 3rd c.BC: Antigonus Carystius Paradoxographus “Mirabilia” 89}}; especially of persons drinking, **drink in long draughts, quaff**, μέθυ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1200; ἄμυστιν [prev. author] “Cyc.” 417}}; τὴν.. τοῦ Πραμνίου [σπονδήν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 107}}; οἶνον ἐκ.. λεπαστῆς Telecl{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 24 (Lyric poetry); ἀπνευστί 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 74.14, etc.}}: with _accusative_ of the cup, δέπας μεστὸν.. ἕλκουσι γνάθοις ἀπαύστοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 237, compare 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 56.7, +others}}; so ἕ. μαστόν **suck** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 987}}; **inhale**, ὀσμήν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Antigonus Carystius Paradoxographus “Mirabilia” 89}}; of roots, **draw up** nourishment, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.6.10: _metaphorically_, χανδὸν καὶ ἀμυστὶ τῶν μαθηυάτων ἕ. 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Vitae Sophistarum” p.474D.}}
**A.II.5** **draw** from a receptacle, ἐξ ἑκάστου κιβωτίου πινάκιον ἕν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 64.1.}}
**A.II.6** ἕ. βίοτον, ζόαν, **drag out** a weary life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 207 (Lyric poetry), “Ph.” 1535 (Lyric poetry)}}; προφάσιας ἕ. **keep making** excuses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.86; πάσας τε προφάσεις.. ἕλκουσι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 727}}; ἕ. χρόνους **make long**, in prosody, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor “Proll. Heph.” p.83C.: hence _intransitive_, ἐπὶ τοσοῦτο λέγεται ἑλκύσαι τὴν σύστασιν..}} that the conflict **dragged on, lasted**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.167, compare “PHib.” 1.83.9 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, τῶν ἐγκλημάτων εἱκλυσμένων πλείονα χρόνον {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 2.281 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; also of a person, ἑλκόμενος καὶ μόγις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 350d.}}
**A.II.7** ἕ. κόρδακα **dance** in **long, measured** steps, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 540}}; ἓν τουτὶ (σχῆμα) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pax” 328.}}
**A.II.8** **draw to oneself, attract**, of the magnet, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 567; by spells, τινὰ ποτὶ δῶμα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.17, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.18, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.4.40, etc.}}; πείθειν καὶ ἑ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 458d; ἐχθροὺς ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτόν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.59}}; **draw on**, ἐπὶ ἡδονάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 238a; εἰς τυραννίδας ἕ. τὰς πολιτείας [prev. author] “R.” 568c}}:—_passive_, **to be drawn on** as by a spell, ἴυγγι δ᾽ ἕλκομαι ἦτορ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.35; πρὸς φιλοσοφίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 494e.}}
**A.II.9** of things weighed, ἕ. σταθμὸν τάλαντα δέκα **draw down** the balance, i.e. **weigh** ten talents, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.50, compare 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 116: _absolutely_, τὸ δ᾽ ἂν ἑλκύσῃ}} whatever it **weigh**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.65}}; πλεῖον ἕ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Minos” 316a.}}
**A.II.9.b** ἕ. τὰς ψήφους **cast up** the account, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.37 (3rd c.BC), “PHib.” 1.17.25 (3rd c.BC).}}
**A.II.10** **draw** or **derive** from a source, ἐντεῦθεν εἵλκυσεν ἐπὶ τὴν.. τέχνην τὸ πρός φορον αὐτῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 270a, compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.207a; τὸ γένος ἀπό τινος 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 11.9.3}}; **assume**, μείζω φαντασίαν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 32.10.5; ὁ ἄρτος ἕλκει χρῶμα κάλλιστον 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.113c.}}
**A.II.11** ἑλκύσαι πλίνθους **make** bricks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.179, compare “PPetr.” 3p.137}}; ἕ. λάγανον Chrysipp. Tyan. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.647e.}}
**A.II.12** αἱ θυρίδες ἕλκουσι the win dows **draw in** air, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Ventis” 29.}}
**A.II.13** ἕ. ἑαυτόν, expressing some kind of athletic exercise, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 135d.}}
**B** _middle_, ἕ. χαίτας ἐκ κεφαλῆς **tear one's** hair, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.15}}; ἀσσοτέρω πυρὸς ἕλκετο δίφρον **drew his** chair nearer to the fire, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.506, compare 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.26.}}
**B.2** **draw to oneself, scrape up, amass**, τιμάς, ἄφενος ἕλκεσθαι, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 30.}}
**B.3** ἕλκεσθαι στάθμας περισσᾶς in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.90}}, means literal, **to drag at** too great a line, i.e.**grasp** more than one's due-- but whence the metaphor is taken remains unexplained.
**C** _passive_, **to be drawn** or **wrenched**, νῶτα.. ἑλκόμενα στερεῶς, of wrestlers, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.715}}; of the nails, to be **curved**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.48}}; **to close in** when the core is removed, of the timber of certain trees, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.5.2.}}
**C.2** **to be drawn** or **to flow at a place**, of streams, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 702; πρὸς ἀντολίην ἕ. αἶα 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 1086.}}
**C.3** **to be drawn** or **contracted**, εἱλκύσθη ἐπὶ τὰ δεξιὰ τράχηλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.14.}}

1671:
1. **Hellas **, a city of Thessaly, founded by Hellen, {{8th/7th c.BC|Iliad by Homer}}
2. **that part of Thessaly in which the Myrmidons dwelt **, also called Phthiotis, {{8th/7th c.BC|Homer}}
3. **Northern Greece **, as opposite to Peloponnesus, {{8th/7th c.BC|Odyssey by Homer}}
4. later, the name for **Greece **, from the South to Epirus and Thessaly inclusively, [{{8th/7th c.BC|Hesiod}}, {{Refs 5th c.BC|Herdotus Historicus}}], etc.
5. as _adjective_ with a fem. _Substantive_ **Hellenic, Greek **, {{Refs 5th c.BC|Herdotus Historicus}}, etc. (Middle Liddel)

1672:
1. **Hellen **, son of Deucalion, {{8th/7th c.BC|Hesiod}}
2. the Ἕλληνες of {{8th/7th c.BC|Homer}} are **the Thessalian tribe of which Hellen was the reputed chief ** (compare Ἑλλάς 1), {{8th/7th c.BC|Iliad by Homer}}
3. later, Ἕλληνες was the _regularly_ name for **Greeks **, opposite to βάρβαροι, {{Refs 5th c.BC|Herdotus Historicus}}, etc.
4. later still, of **Gentiles **, Opp. to Jews, {{Refs 1st c.AD|New Testament}}
5. as _adjective_ = Ἑλληνικός, {{Refs 5th c.BC|Thucydides}}, etc.:—;even with a fem. _Substantive_, [{{6th/5th c.BC|Aeschulus Tragicus}}, {{Refs 5th c.BC|Euripides}}] (Middle Liddel)

1673:
1. **Hellenic, Greek **, {{Refs 5th c.BC|Herdotus Historicus}}, attic
2. τὸ Ἑλληνικόν **the Greeks ** collectively, {{Refs 5th c.BC|Herdotus Historicus}}; **the Greek soldiery **, {{5th/4th c.BC|Xenophon Historicus}}
3. τὰ Ἑλληνικά **the history of Grecian affairs **, {{Refs 5th c.BC|Thucydides}}
4. **like the Greeks **, [{{Refs 5th c.BC|Euripides}}, {{5th/4th c.BC|Aristophanes Comicus}}]:—;_adverb_ -κῶς, **in Greek fashion **, {{Refs 5th c.BC|Herdotus Historicus}} (Middle Liddel)

1677:
** ἐλλογ-έω**,
(λόγος) ={ἐν λόγῳ τιθέναι}, **reckon, put to an account,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Philm.18; τινί “PRyl.” 243.11 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.:—_passive_, **to be reckoned in,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 9(1).61.37 (Daulis, 2nd c.AD), “PStrassb.” 1.32.10 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **impute**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 140.32 (2nd c.AD)}}:—_passive_, {{NT|NT.Rom.5.13.}}

1679:
** ἐλπίζω**,
_Attic dialect_ _future_ **-ῐῶ**{{LXX+NT+4th c.BC+|LXX.Psa.43(44).7, NT.Rom.15.12; ἐλπίσω 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.656 (ἐλπίσω in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 187 is _aorist_ _subjunctive_)}}: _aorist_ ἤλπισα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.24, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1175 (Lyric poetry), etc. (ἤλπιζα (sic) “IG” 3.1350)}}: _perfect_ ἤλπικα {{NT+3rd c.BC+|NT.John.5.45, (προ-) 3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 27.8}}: _pluperfect_ ἠλπίκειν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 17, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 71, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.5.1}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Αιβυκή” 115 (assuming variant), “Supp.epigram” 2.461 (Histria, 1st c.BC)}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἠλπίσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1105, “APl.” 4.222 (Parmenio Epigrammaticus)}}: _perfect_ ἤλπισμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.40:— _Attic dialect_ form of ἔλπομαι, used also by 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}, **hope for**, or rather (in earlier writers) **look for, expect**:—Constr.: with _accusative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 589, “Ch.” 539, etc., τι παρά τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.17, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.102}}: frequently with a dependent clause in _infinitive_, **hope to** do, or **hope** or **expect that**..; with _future_ _infinitive_, ἐ. μιν ἀποθανέεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.143, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.6, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.71, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.2}}; ἐ. τὴν Εὐρώπην δουλώσεσθαι (variant{-ασθαι}) {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.21}}: with _aorist_ _infinitive_, ἐ. ποτὲ δεῖξαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 629; ἤλπιζον ἑλεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 7.6: also with ἄν, οὐδαμὰ ἐλπίζων ἂν ἡμίονον τεκέειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.151, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.53}}; the _infinitive_ may be omitted, ἔκλυον ἃν.. οὐδ᾽ ἂν ἤλπισ᾽ αὐδάν (i.e. κλύειν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1281}}; also ἐ. ὅπως.., with _future_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 1051, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 963}}:—_passive_, τὸ μηδαμὰ ἐλπισθὲν ἥξειν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “OC” 1105; ὁ ἐλπισθεὶς αὐτοκράτωρ “POxy.” 1021.6 (1st c.AD).}}
**2** of evils, **look for, fear**, in same _construction_, δύστανον ἐ. αἶσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 111 (Lyric poetry); ἔξοδον ὀλεθρίαν Αἴαντος ἐλπίζει φέρειν [prev. author] “Aj.” 799, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.70; τουτί.. τὸ κακὸν οὐδέποτ᾽ ἤλπισα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 956; ἐ. πάγχυ ἀπολέεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.12; θῆράς σφε τὸν δύστηνον ἐλπίζει κτανεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 348: with μή}} followed by _aorist_ _subjunctive_, οὐδαμὰ ἐλπίσας μή κοτε ἐλάσῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.77; οὐκ ἂν ἤλπισε μή κοτέ τις ἀναβαίη [prev. author] 8.53.}}
**3** with _present_ _infinitive_, **deem, suppose that**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 11.2; ἐλπίζων εἶναι.. ὀλβιώτατος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30; ἐλπίζων σιτοδείην τε εἶναι ἰσχυρὴν.. καὶ τὸν λεὼν τετρῦσθαι [prev. work] 22}}; οἰκότα ἐλπίζων{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 27, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 76, “Ch.” 187}}; βοῦν ἢ λέοντ᾽ ἤλπιζες ἐντείνειν βρόχοι; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 720; ἐλπίζει δυνατὸς εἶναι ἄρχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 573c; ὅστις ἐλπίζει θεοὺς.. χαίρειν ἀπαρχαῖς “Tragica Adespota” 118.2: sometimes of future events, τίς ἂν ἤλπισεν ἁμαρτήσεσθαί τινα τῶν πολιτῶν τοιαύτην ἁμαρτία; 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.27; οὐδὲν.. ποιήσειν ἐλπίζων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.7.}}
**4** with _dative_, **hope in**.., τῇ τύχῃ {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.97; ὀνόματι NT.Matt.12.21}}: also with Preps., ἔν τισι {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.18.5}}; πρός τι{{LXX+NT|LXX.Judg.20.36; ἐ. εἴς τινα NT.John.5.45, +others; ἐπί τινι NT.Rom.15.12, +others; ἐπί τινα NT.1Pet.3.5.}}

1680:
** ἐλπ-ίς**, ίδος, ἡ,
(see. ἔλπω) **hope, expectation** (δόξα μελλόντων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 644c), ἔτι γὰρ καὶ ἐλπίδος αἶσα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.101, 19.84; personified, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 96: _plural_, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.49, etc.}}; πολλῶν ῥαγεισῶν ἐλπίδων after the wreck of many **hopes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 505; ἔτι ἐν αὐτοῖς εἰσὶν ἐλπίδες, νέοι γάρ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328d; κεναῖσιν ἐλπίσιν θερμαίνεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 478}}; **expectancy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 771 (_plural_), “OC” 1749 (Lyric poetry, _plural_), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.32 (_plural_), etc.:}} —Constr., in _Attic dialect_, with _genitive_ both of subject and object, as (where both are conjoined) Πελοποννησίων τὴν ἐλπίδα τοῦ ναυτικοῦ **the hope of** the P.**in** their navy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89; also αἱ τῶν Ἑλλήνων ἐς ὑμᾶς ἐλπίδες [prev. author] 3.14}}; ὑμέτεραι ἐλπίδες,= ἐς ὑμᾶς, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.69}}; ἐλπίδ᾽ ἔχω, ={ἐλπίζω}, with _future_ _infinitive_, μὴ οὐ δώσειν δίκην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.11, etc.}}: with _aorist_ _infinitive_, κλέος εὑρέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.111: with ὡς}} and _future_ _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 385; ὥστε μὴ θανεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 52; περὶ τῆς ἐμαυτοῦ ψυχῆς οὐ πολλὰς ἐλπίδας ἔχω 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.27}}; ἐν ἐλπίδι εἰμί, with _future_ _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.46; ἐν ἐλπίσι καλαῖς γενόμενος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 40}}; ἐλπίς [ἐστί] μοι with _accusative_ and _future_ _infinitive_ or _aorist_, ἐλπίς τις αὐτὸν ἥξειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 679; τοσοῦτόν γ᾽ ἐστί μοι τῆς ἐλπίδος, τὸν ἄνδρα.. προσμεῖναι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 836; πλείων ἐλπὶς φιλίαν ἢ ἔχθραν γενέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 232e}}: with _present_ _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Sph.” 250e: followed by ὡς.., 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 487; ἐς ἐλπίδα ἐλθεῖν τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.56; ἐπ᾽ ἐλπίδας ἀφανεῖς καθίστασθαι [prev. author] 5.103; ἐλπίδα λαβεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.7}}; ἐλπίδας μεγάλας ἔν τινι ἔχειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.4.25, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.121}}; τίν᾽ ὑπάγεις μ᾽ ἐς ἐλπίδ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 826}}; ἐλπίδας ἐμποιεῖν ἀνθρώποις, ὑποθεῖναί τισι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.19, “HG” 4.8.28; ἐλπίδας μεγίστας παρέχεται ποιῆσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 193d}}; ἐλπίδα or ἐλπίδας ὑπογράφειν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.65U., 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.36.1; ἀποκεκομμένης τῆς ἐλπίδος [prev. author] 3.63.8, compare 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1272}}; ἐκτὸς ἐλπίδος beyond **hope**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 330; ἀπ᾽ ἐλπίδος πεσεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 999}}; παρ᾽ ἐλπίδα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 899, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 882: proverbial, πεινῶμεν ἐπὶ ταῖς ἐλπίσιν 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 123.7; κάπτοντες αὔρας ἐλπίδας σιτούμενοι 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 10.7; αἱ δ᾽ ἐλπίδες βόσκουσι τοὺς κενούς 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 42.}}
**2** **object of hope, a hope**, Ὀρέστης, ἐ. δόμων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 776}}; ὑμεῖς, ἡ μόνη ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.57}}; Εὔτυχος, ἡ γονέων ἐ. {{Refs|“IG” 3.1311.}}
**3** **reason to expect** or **believe**, πολλὴ ἐ. κτήσασθαι, νοητὸν εἶναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 67b, “Lg.” 898d.}}
**II** **anxious thought on the future, boding**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1434, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 267, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 859, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 644c.}}

1684:
** ἐμβαίνω**,
_future_ -βήσομαι: _perfect_ -βέβηκα; _Epic dialect_ _participle_ ἐμβεβᾰώς, -υῖα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.199, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 12, etc.:}}_aorist 2_ ἐνέβη; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔμβη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.656; dual _imperative_ ἔμβητον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.403}}:—**step in**, μή τις.. ἐμβήῃ let none **step in** (so as to interfere), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|16.94: with _dative_, ποταμῷ οὐκ ἔστιν δὶς τῷ αὐτῷ ἐμβῆναι 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 91; εἰς πηλόν [prev. author] 5; ἐμβέβακεν ἴχνεσιν πατρός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.12.}}
**2** **go on, go quickly**, ἔμβητον, says Antilochus to his horses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.403; ἔμβα}}**advance!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 113 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** **embark** on a ship, ἐρέται δ᾽ ἐν ἑκάστη πεντήκοντα ἐμβέβασαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.720; τότε δ᾽ ἔμβη νηΐ Πύλονδε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.656, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.311}}; ἐς ἕτερον πλοῖον ἐ. (variant for{ἐσβ-}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.29, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.18 (variant), 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.40, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 243c: with _accusative_, λέμβον ἐ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.9.11}}: _absolutely_, **embark**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 455 (troch.), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 188, etc.}}: generally, **step into, mount**, εἰς τὸ φορεῖον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 26}}: _perfect_, **to be mounted on**, ἵπποισι καὶ ἅρμασιν ἐμβεβαῶτα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.199; ἐπ᾽ ἀπήνης ἐμβεβώς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 803: also with _accusative_, Τροίαν Ἰλιάδ᾽ ἐμβεβαῶτα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 922 (Lyric poetry); στέγην τήνδ᾽ ἐμβεβῶτες [prev. author] “Cyc.” 92.}}
**4** **step upon**, τῷ δ᾽ ἐγὼ ἐμβαίνων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.164; πεδίλοις ἐμβεβαυῖα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 12; τοῖσδ᾽ ἁλουργέσιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 946}}; δαίμων ἐνέβη Περσῶν γενεᾷ **trampled upon** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 911 (_anapaest meter_); μὴ᾽ μβαινε τῷ δυστυχοῦντι 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 356}}: _absolutely_, **tread on** one's **toes**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 15.6}}; compare βοῦς viii.
**5** **enter upon**, ἐς τόνδε χρησμόν uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1567; εἰς κίνδυνον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.15: with _accusative_, ἐ. κέλευθον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 989 (Lyric poetry).}}
**5.b** _metaphorically_, **enter upon, embark in**, μεγαλανορίαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.44; τῷ ἐπιτηδεύατι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 252e}}; ἐν αὐτοῖς τοῖς δεινοῖς ἐμβεβηκώς **embarked, engaged in**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.248}}; **light upon**, εἰς ἀρχήν τε καὶ τύπον τῆς δικαιοσύνης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 443c}}: _absolutely_, **enter upon office,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 5(1).1390.31 (from Andania).}}
**6** rarely with _genitive_, **step upon**, νᾶος {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 19; γῆς ὅρων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 400.}}
**7** Poets, with _accusative_ of the instrument of motion, ὄχοις.. ἐμβεβὼς πόδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 672}}; ἐς ἄντλον ἐμβήσει (_2nd pers._ _singular_) πόδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 168.}}
**8** **to be fixed** or **fastened**, κατά τι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.81}}; **to be fixed in**, εἰς ἐμπυελίδας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 2.3.}}
**9** ={ἐμβατεύω}{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|11, “SIG” 364.75 (Ephesus, 3rd c.BC).}}
**II** causal in _aorist 1_ ἐνέβησα, **make to step in, put in**, ἐν δὲ τὰ μῆλα.. ἐβήσαμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.4; δίφρον ἐμβῆσαί τινα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 845, compare “Cyc.” 467; ἐ. τὰν ἀρχάν “Schwyzer” 485.9 (Thespiae, 3rd c.BC): _metaphorically_, ἐμβῆσαί τινα ἐς φροντίδα}} **plunge** him into anxiety, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.46.}}
**III** _intransitive_, **step, march** or **dance**, ὀρθῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.108c; πρὸς ῥυθμόν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 10.}}

1685:
** ἐμβάλλω**,
_future_ -βᾰλῶ: _perfect_ -βέβληκα: _aorist 2_ ἐνέβᾰλον (_passive_ is mostly supplied by ἐμπίπτω):— **throw in**, τινὰ πόντῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.258}}; μιν.. χερσὶν᾽ Ἀχιλλῆος θεὸς ἔμβαλεν **let** him **fall into** Achilles' hands,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|21.47; ἐ. νιν βροτοῦ ἀνέρος εὐνῇ 18.85; ἐ. τινὰ εἰς τὸ βάραθρον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 574, “Nu.” 1450; εἰς τὸ δεσμωτήριον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.14; ἐ. τινὰ εἰς συμφοράς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.10; εἰς ἀτυχίας 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.79; εἰς αἰσχύνην καὶ ἀδικίαν 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 3.7; εἰς ὑποψίαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 23; ἐς γραφάς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 679, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.72, etc.; εἰς ἀπορίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 20a; εἰς ἔχθραν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.70.}}
**2** of things, ἵπποις χαλινοὺς ἐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 551, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 6.7 (_passive_), 9.9, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.394; πώλοις ἡνίας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1424; ἐ. ψήφους εἰς τὸν καδίσκον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.21}}; ἐ. μοχλόν (i.e. εἰς τὴν θύραν) {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 7.1.12}}; ἐ. σῖτον (i.e. εἰς τὴν φάτνην) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.1.38}}; τοῖς ὑποζυγίοις ἐ. **throw** food **to**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 4.8}}; simply, **lay** or **put in**, [ἱμάντα] οἱ ἔμβαλε χερσίν **put** it **into** his hands, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.218}}; ἐνέβαλον τῶν χρημάτων [εἰς τὸ κανοῦν] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1304a3, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 11.5}}; **hand in, submit** a petition, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3P. 39 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; ἐ. τὴν χεῖρά τινι **slide** one's hand **into** another's, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 554}}; ἔμβαλλε χεῖρα δεξιάν as a pledge of good faith, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1181, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 754}}; ἔμβαλλε χειρὸς πίστιν, to which Neoptolemus answers—ἐμβάλλω μενεῖν I **give my pledge** to remain, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 813 (troch.).}}
**3** frequently of the mind, ἐνὶ φρεσὶν ἐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.10 (compare below 111.2); εἰς νοῦν τινί 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 3}}; ἐ. ἵμερον, μένος τινί, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.139, 16.529}}; ἐ. νεῖκός τισι **to throw in** strife between them, {{Refs|4.444}}; τισὶ λύσσαν ἐρισμοῦ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Timo 28.3; ἐ. λόγον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 344d; βουλὴν ἐ. περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.18 (and _absolutely_, ἐ. τινὶ περί τινος}} **to give** one **advice** on a thing, {{Refs|[prev. work] 5.5.43}} (nisi addendum [βουλήν])); ἐ. πρᾶγμα εἰς γέλωτα καὶ λοιδορίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.75.}}
**4** **throw upon** or **against**, νηῒ κεραυνόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.415; δαλὸν νήεσσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.320; πέτρον στέρνῳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.68; [Ἀχαιοὺς] πέτραις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1129 (Lyric poetry); πῆχυν στέρνοις [prev. author] “Or.” 1466 (Lyric poetry); λίθον τινὶ εἰς κεφαλήν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.26; πληγάς τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.11, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 66}}; so ἐμβαλέτω ἰσχυρότατα (i.e. πληγάς) **let him lay on**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 8.4}}; ἐ. ἕλκεα **to inflict** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 111}}; ἐ. πῦρ **set** fire **to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.53}}; ἐ. ῥήγεα **lay on** blankets, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.298: _metaphorically_, ἐ. φόβον τινί}} **strike** fear into him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10. ; ἄταν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 316 (Lyric poetry)}}; φροντίδας variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.2}}; **impose**, ἔργα εἰς τὴν γῆν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PTeb.” 37.7 (_passive_, 1st c.BC)}}; of a fine, {{Refs|“BCH” 8.307 (from Delos).}}
**5** ἐ. ὦμον **put** one's shoulder **to** the work, in archery, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 2.}}
**6** **put into** its place, **to set** a broken or dislocated limb, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 24 (_passive_), “Art.” 1, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 685b6.}}
**7** in Medicine texts, **put in**, ἀμυχάς, διαίρεσιν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 7.4, 2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 45.24.4.}}
**8** **graft** a tree, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.15 (_passive_)}}; but simply, **plant**, τὰ φυτά {{Refs|“IG” 12(7).62.29.}}
**9** ἐ. τινί (i.e. μάρμαρον) **to throw at** another, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.383.}}
**10** **insert** a word or a letter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 343d, “Cra.” 414c, +others; εἰς κωμῳδίαν στίχον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.334f.}}
**11** ἐ. οἰκίαν τινί **bring** it **down upon** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 511, compare “Nu.” 1489.}}
**12** τάφρον ἐ. **make** a trench, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 27, “Mar.” 15.}}
**13** **pay, contribute**, ἀργύριον {{Refs|“IG” 7.235.13 (from Oropus)}}; τροφάν {{Refs|“GDI” 1884.12 (Delph.).}}
**14** **denounce** an offender, ἐς τὰν βωλάν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 527.103 (Dreros, 3rd c.BC).}}
**15** **intercalate** a month, {{Refs|“IG” 12.76.53.}}
**II** _intransitive_ (i.e. στρατόν), **make an inroad** or **invasion**, variant for{ἐσβ}. in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.125, 5.15, 9.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.29; in full, ἐ. στράτευμα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 583, 1024}}: _metaphorically_, **attack**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 165d.}}
**II.b** generally, **burst, rush in**, ἐμβάλλειν εἰς τὴν ἀγοράν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.164, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 5, etc.}}; **embark** upon, ἐμβάλωμεν εἰς ἄλλον λόγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 962: with _dative_, εἰκασίαις Hierocl. p.37 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus; βίβλοις μακραῖς καὶ δυσελίκτοις 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.227b.}}
**II.2** **strike** a ship **with the ram** (ἔμβολος {{Refs|1.3)}}, **charge** or **ram** it, νηΐ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.84, +others, compare 7.10}}.; ἐ. ταῖς λοιπαῖς (i.e. ναυσί) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.14}}; ξυνετύγχανε.. διὰ τὴν στενοχωρίαν τὰ μὲν ἄλλοις ἐμβεβληκέναι τὰ δὲ αὐτοὺς ἐμβεβλῆσθαι on one side **had charged** others, on the other **had been charged** themselves, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.70.}}
**II.2.b** of water, ἐ. τοῖς ὄρεσι **to dash against** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.28: _absolutely_, τὸ ὕδωρ ἐμβαλὸν τὰ χωρία ἐλυμήνατο 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 55.11.}}
**II.3** κώπῃς ἐ. (i.e. χεῖρας) **lay oneself** to the oars, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.129, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.201}}; ἐ. alone, **pull hard**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 602, “Ra.” 206, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.13.}}
**II.4** of a river, **empty itself**, εἰς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 113c.}}
**III** _middle_, **throw in what is one's own**, ὅρκον εἰς τὸν ἐχῖνον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.65, compare 27.51}}: _absolutely_, **draw lots**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 1006.3 (Cos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**III.2** _metaphorically_, μή μοι φύξιν ἐμβάλλεο θυμῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.447}}; μῆτιν ἐ. θ{{Refs 4th c.BC+|. 23.313; εἰς τὸν νοῦν ἐμβάλλεσθαί τι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.68}} (later in _active_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PTaur.” 4.9); τὸ καρτερὸν ἐμβαλόμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.21 (compare above 1.3).}}
**III.3** with _genitive_, ἐμβάλλεσθε τῶν λαγῴων **fall upon** the hare's flesh, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1312.}}
**III.4** **put on board ship,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PHib. 1.152 (3rd c.BC), “POxy.” 1292.3 (1st c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.5, etc.}}
**III.5** **set to work upon**, τῇ γεωργίᾳ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PStrassb.” 111.3 (3rd c.BC).}}
**IV** _passive_, **to be dashed against**: of ships, **charge** (see.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|above 11.2), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.34, 70.}}

1687:
** ἐμβᾰτ-εύω**,
**step in** or **on, frequent, haunt**: with _accusative_, of tutelary gods, νῆσος.. ἣν ὁ φιλόχορος Πὰν ἐμβατεύει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 449, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 595; Πὰν Πελασγικὸν Ἄργος ἐμβατεύων 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 321; ἵνα Διόνυσος ἐμβατεύει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 679 (Lyric poetry): with _dative_, ὁ -εύων τῷ χωρίῳ δαίμων 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.77}}: with _genitive_, in simple sense, **set foot upon**, μήτ᾽ ἐμβατεύειν πατρίδος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 825}}: _absolutely_, **enter** a sacred cave, {{Refs|“OGI” 530.15 (from Iasus).}}
**II** ἐ. κλήρους χθονός **enter on, come into possession of**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 876, cf. LXX.Josh.19.49: more frequently ἐ. εἰς τὴν ναῦν}} **enter on possession of** the vessel, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.6; εἰς τὴν οὐσίαν [prev. author] 44.19; εἰς τὸ χωρίον 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.3}}: _absolutely_, **enter on an inheritance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Elephantine Papyri 2.14 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, νέων ψυχάς {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 4.5.}}
**III** **mount, cover**, of the male, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 39.}}
**IV** **to be initiated** into the mysteries, {{NT|“Jahresh.” 15.46 (from Notium), cf. NT.Col.2.18.}}

1688:
** ἐμβῐβ-άζω**,
_Attic dialect_ _future_ -βιβῶ, causal of ἐμβαίνω, **set in** or **on**, τινὰ ὡς εἰς ὄχημα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 41e; ἐ. εἰς ἴχνος [prev. author] “Tht.” 193c}}:—_passive_, **to be put into, take** a bath, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.37.16.}}
**2** **put on board ship, cause to embark**, ἄνδρας ἐς κελήτιον (variant for{ἐσ-}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.53; εἰς πλοῖα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.3.1; ἐ. ναυσίν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 7, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 8.3}}: _absolutely_, **put on board**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.8, etc.}}:—_middle_, ἐμβιβασάμενος αὐτοὺς εἰς τὰς ναῦς {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 5.1.19.}}
**3** **lead, guide** to a thing, εἰς τὸ λῷστον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 856; εἰς τὴν δικαιοσύνην τοὺς οἰκέτας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 14.4; εἰς λόγους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.97; εἰς ἀπέχθειαν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.38.1; εἰς μέτρα ἐ. χρησμούς 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 6.11; τὴν ἀπάδουσαν εἰς τὸ μέλος [prev. author] “Im.” 2.1}}; τοῖς ἀνθρωπίνοις πάθεσιν τὸν θεὸν ἐ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.416f.}}
**4** **set** a dislocated joint, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 7.}}
**5** ἐ. τινὰ εἰς.. **put in possession of..,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PFlor. 55.31 (1st c.AD), etc.}}
**6** _intransitive_, οἱ τῆς σταδιαίας πάλης ἐμβιβάζοντες {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.22.4.}}

1689:
** ἐμ-βλέπω**,
_perfect_ ἐμβέβλοφα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PLond.” 1.42.21 (2nd c.BC)}}:—**look in the face, look at**, τινὶ τοῖς ὀφθαλμοῖς {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 155c, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.69; ἐ. εἰς τὸν ὀφθαλμόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus 1.132e, etc.: with _accusative_, ἐμβλέπω σε, παῖ, “Comica Adespota” 17.7 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, compare 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.68, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.3, NT.Mark.8.25: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.10, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1175a9.}}
**b** ἐ. εἰς **consider**, τὰ πετεινὰ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ {{NT|NT.Matt.6.26}}; **look into** a matter, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 28.15 (2nd c.BC).}}
**2** simply, **look**, ποῖ ἐμβλέψασα..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 995}}; δεινὸν ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 535e, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 34, etc.}}; πῦρ ἐ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.28; ἱρὰ ἐς λῷον -οντα 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.80.}}

1690:
** ἐμβρῑμ-άομαι**
(_active_ only in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus)}}, with _aorist_ _middle_ et _passive_, **snort in**, ἵππους ἐν ἀμπυκτῆρσιν ἐμβριμωμένας, of horses, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 461, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nec.” 20.}}
**2** of persons, **to be deeply moved**, τῷ πνεύματι, ἐν ἑαυτῷ, {{NT|NT.John.11.33, 38.}}
**II** **admonish urgently, rebuke**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1099: with _dative_ _person_, LXX.Dan.11.30, NT.Matt.9.30, “Marc.” 1.43.}}

1691:
** ἐγώ**,
I: _pronoun_ of the first person:—_Epic dialect_ mostly **ἐγών** before vowels (so in _Doric dialect_, before consonants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 85, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 81, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 748, 754), rarely in Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 932 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱών** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 51.4}}:— strengthened **ἔγωγε**, **I at least, for my part, indeed, for myself** (more frequently in _Attic dialect_ than in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}: _Doric dialect_ **ἐγώνγα** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 51, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 736, “Lys.” 986, _dative_ ἐμίνγα “IG” 22.1126.7 (Amphict. Delph.)}}: _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱώνγα** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 21}}; **ἱώνει** {{Refs|Ead. 10}}; **ἰώγα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 898}}: _Laconian dialect_ and _Tarentum dialect_ **ἐγώνη**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 255.29.}}
**II** oblique cases from a different root, _genitive_ ἐμοῦ, _enclitic_ μο; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ἐμέο, ἐμεῦ, μευ, also ἐμέθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.525, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 177 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμεθεν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.7; ἐμεῖο “IG” 3.1337; μεθέν 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 20}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμέος, ἐμεῦς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 144}}; _Boeotian dialect_ ἐμοῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 37; also ἐμῶς, ἐμίο, ἐμίω, ἐμίως 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.17:}} — _dative_ ἐμοί, _enclitic_ μοι (which may be compared with Sanskrit _genitive_ **me** in κλῦθί μοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.115, +others); ὅ μοι πόσις “Schwyzer” 683 (5th c.AD: Cypria)}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 99, “AJA” 29.461 (Rhodian, 5th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 733, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.30}}; _Tarentum dialect_ ἐμίνη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13: _accusative_ ἐμέ}}, _enclitic_ μ; {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria μι “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 59,60 H.}}
**III** dual, _nominative_ and _accusative_, **νῶι**, **we two**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.34, etc.}}; _accusative_ νῶιν Zenod.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.377}}; _Attic dialect_ νώ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 278b (also 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.219, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.475); νῶε 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 39, 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 5: _genitive_, _dative_ νῶι; νῷν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 3}}; νῶι _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 773}}; νῶιν, ={ἡμῖν}, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.213, etc.}}
**IV** _plural_, _nominative_ ἡμεῖς (ἡμέες _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.6, +others, rejected by 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.1)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.303, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.144}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμές {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 65, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 42, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168:— _genitive_ ἡμῶν (also ἥμων 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 130.23)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.112, etc.; ἡμείων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.170, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.46}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἀμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 88, “Milet.” 3 No.152.29}}; ἄμμων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work]74, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 66}}; ἁμῶν [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 266, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.158}}; Cretan dialect, _Boeotian dialect_ ἁμίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 528.5, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.21:— _dative_ ἡμῖν, in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus also ἡμίν}} (ῐ) (or ἧμιν Aristarch.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.214, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3)}}; also rarely in Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 37, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 386 (uncertain)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμῐν, ἄμμῐ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.384, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 80, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.155, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 156 (Lyric poetry), “Milet.” 3 No.152; ἄμμεσιν 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 100}}; _Doric dialect_ also ἁμίν or ἇμιν, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 77, 78, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 347 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1081}}; with ῑ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 821, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.145:— _accusative_ ἡμᾶς (also ἧμᾰς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.372)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.211, “SIG” 273.25 (Milet., 4th c.BC)}}; ἥμεας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.294 (cf. Hdn.Gr. 2.140)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.59, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 115}}, Theocr.8.25; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 1 (Abu Simbel, 6th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 173, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 759 codices, “Lys.” 95.}}—On these dialectic varieties, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 50}} _ff._ (Cf. Sanskrit _ahám_ (ἐγών), _accusative_ _plural_ **asmā´n**; for νώ cf. Sanskrit _nau_):—frequently in answers, as an affirmative, especially in form ἔγωγε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1248, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 149b, etc.}}; οὗτος ἐ. here am {{Refs 5th c.BC+|I, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; ὅδ᾽ ἐκεῖνος ἐ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 138 (Lyric poetry)}}; rarely with _Article_, τὸν ἐμέ **myself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 166a, “Sph.” 239b (but ὁ ἐ.}} **the Self, the Ego**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 444)}}; τίς ὢν οὗτος ὁ ἐγὼ τυγχάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1119a; τί ἐστι φίλο; ἄλλος ἐ. 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.166 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}; τί τοῦτ᾽ ἐμο; ἡμῖν τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ; Latin _quid mea hoc refert_ ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 498, etc.}}; ἐγ; in a question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1336, +others}}; ἡμεῖς **the self**, ἔνθα δὴ ἡμεῖς μάλιστα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.1.7.}}

1692:
** ἐμέω**
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.11}}, _imperfect_ ἤμουν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 351, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.20}}, _Ionic dialect_ ἤμεον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.88}}: _future_ ἐμέσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.15, _Attic dialect_ ἐμῶ (ἐνεξ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Polyzelus Comicus 4}}: _future_ _middle_ ἐμέομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος ἀνθρώπου” 5, ἐμοῦμαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 730}}: _aorist_ ἤμεσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.26.έ, etc., (ἐξ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 6}}, _infinitive_ ἐμέσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.133}}; _Epic dialect_ ἔμεσσα (ἀπ-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.437 (probably ἐξήμεσσα should be restored for -ήμησα in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 497}}; ὑπερ-έμησα occurs in the Mss. of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.17)}}: _perfect_ ἐμήμεκα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 21, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 17.37}}: _pluperfect_ ἐμημέκεε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.42, ἐμημέκει 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.7:}} —_passive_, _future_ ἐμεθήσομαι (ἐξ-) {{LXX|LXX.Job.20.15}}: _aorist_ _infinitive_ ἐμεθῆναι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.219}}: _perfect_ ἐμήμεσμαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 13.22}}:—**vomit, throw up**, αἷμ᾽ ἐμέων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.11, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.88; ἐμοῦσα θρόμβους 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 184; ἰόν [prev. work] 730}}: _absolutely_, **vomit, be sick**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.133, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.20; ἐμέειν ἀπὸ συρμαϊσμοῦ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 40}}; ἐ. πτίλῳ **to make oneself sick** with a feather, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 587.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **throw up a flood of words**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Vitae Sophistarum” p.488 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus (ϝεμε-}}, cf. Sanskrit _vámiti_ 'vomit', Latin _vomo_, **vomitus**, Lithuanian _vémti_, etc.)

1696:
** ἐμμένω**,
_future_ **-μενῶ**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 648, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἐμμεμένηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.5}}:—**abide in** a place, πολὺν χρόνον μελάθροις ἐμμένειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 362.12}}; ἐν τῇ κεφαλῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1120; ἐν τῇ Ἀττικῇ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.17, Epist. 1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 12.22: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.31.}}
**2** **abide by, stand by, cleave to, be true to**, with _dative_, τοῖς ὁρκίοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.106; πιστώμασι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 977, etc.; τῷ κηρύγματι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 351; ὀρθῷ νόμῳ [prev. author] “Aj.” 350; ἐ. ταῖς συνθήκαις καὶ ταῖς σπονδαῖς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.18, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.81; τοῖς νόμοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.16; τῷ τιμήματι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 39b; τῇ δμολογίᾳ [prev. author] “Tht.” 145c, etc.}}; ἐ. τοῖς Καρχηδονίοις **remain constant** to them, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Ἰβηρική” 24}}; ἐ. ἐν ταῖς σπονδαῖς τὸν ἐνιαυτόν Indut. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.118; ἐν τῇ τάξει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 844c; ἐν τῇ φιλοσοφίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.89}}: _absolutely_, **stand fast be faithful**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1241, “PTeb.” 382.22 (1st c.BC).}}
**3** of things, **remain fixed, stand fast, hold good**, εἴ σοί γ᾽ ἅπερ φὴς ἐμμενεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 648}}; μάλα μοι τοῦτ᾽ ἐμμένοι **may** it **remain fixed in** my mind, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 534 (Lyric poetry)}}; εἴ σφι ἔτι ἐμμένει [ἡ φιλίη] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.151; τέσσαρα καὶ δέκα ἔτη ἐνέμειναν αἱ σπονδαί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.2; ἐ. ὁ νόμος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 839c; ἐὰν.. [ὁ λόγος] ἐμμένῃ [prev. author] “Phdr.” 258b}}; τὸ σιδηροφορεῖσθαι τοῖς ἠπειρώταις ἐμμεμένηκεν **continued as a custom**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.5.}}

1698:
** ἐγώ**,
I: _pronoun_ of the first person:—_Epic dialect_ mostly **ἐγών** before vowels (so in _Doric dialect_, before consonants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 85, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 81, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 748, 754), rarely in Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 932 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱών** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 51.4}}:— strengthened **ἔγωγε**, **I at least, for my part, indeed, for myself** (more frequently in _Attic dialect_ than in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}: _Doric dialect_ **ἐγώνγα** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 51, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 736, “Lys.” 986, _dative_ ἐμίνγα “IG” 22.1126.7 (Amphict. Delph.)}}: _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱώνγα** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 21}}; **ἱώνει** {{Refs|Ead. 10}}; **ἰώγα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 898}}: _Laconian dialect_ and _Tarentum dialect_ **ἐγώνη**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 255.29.}}
**II** oblique cases from a different root, _genitive_ ἐμοῦ, _enclitic_ μο; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ἐμέο, ἐμεῦ, μευ, also ἐμέθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.525, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 177 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμεθεν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.7; ἐμεῖο “IG” 3.1337; μεθέν 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 20}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμέος, ἐμεῦς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 144}}; _Boeotian dialect_ ἐμοῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 37; also ἐμῶς, ἐμίο, ἐμίω, ἐμίως 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.17:}} — _dative_ ἐμοί, _enclitic_ μοι (which may be compared with Sanskrit _genitive_ **me** in κλῦθί μοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.115, +others); ὅ μοι πόσις “Schwyzer” 683 (5th c.AD: Cypria)}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 99, “AJA” 29.461 (Rhodian, 5th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 733, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.30}}; _Tarentum dialect_ ἐμίνη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13: _accusative_ ἐμέ}}, _enclitic_ μ; {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria μι “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 59,60 H.}}
**III** dual, _nominative_ and _accusative_, **νῶι**, **we two**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.34, etc.}}; _accusative_ νῶιν Zenod.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.377}}; _Attic dialect_ νώ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 278b (also 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.219, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.475); νῶε 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 39, 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 5: _genitive_, _dative_ νῶι; νῷν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 3}}; νῶι _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 773}}; νῶιν, ={ἡμῖν}, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.213, etc.}}
**IV** _plural_, _nominative_ ἡμεῖς (ἡμέες _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.6, +others, rejected by 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.1)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.303, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.144}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμές {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 65, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 42, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168:— _genitive_ ἡμῶν (also ἥμων 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 130.23)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.112, etc.; ἡμείων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.170, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.46}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἀμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 88, “Milet.” 3 No.152.29}}; ἄμμων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work]74, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 66}}; ἁμῶν [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 266, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.158}}; Cretan dialect, _Boeotian dialect_ ἁμίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 528.5, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.21:— _dative_ ἡμῖν, in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus also ἡμίν}} (ῐ) (or ἧμιν Aristarch.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.214, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3)}}; also rarely in Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 37, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 386 (uncertain)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμῐν, ἄμμῐ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.384, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 80, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.155, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 156 (Lyric poetry), “Milet.” 3 No.152; ἄμμεσιν 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 100}}; _Doric dialect_ also ἁμίν or ἇμιν, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 77, 78, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 347 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1081}}; with ῑ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 821, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.145:— _accusative_ ἡμᾶς (also ἧμᾰς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.372)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.211, “SIG” 273.25 (Milet., 4th c.BC)}}; ἥμεας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.294 (cf. Hdn.Gr. 2.140)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.59, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 115}}, Theocr.8.25; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 1 (Abu Simbel, 6th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 173, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 759 codices, “Lys.” 95.}}—On these dialectic varieties, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 50}} _ff._ (Cf. Sanskrit _ahám_ (ἐγών), _accusative_ _plural_ **asmā´n**; for νώ cf. Sanskrit _nau_):—frequently in answers, as an affirmative, especially in form ἔγωγε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1248, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 149b, etc.}}; οὗτος ἐ. here am {{Refs 5th c.BC+|I, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; ὅδ᾽ ἐκεῖνος ἐ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 138 (Lyric poetry)}}; rarely with _Article_, τὸν ἐμέ **myself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 166a, “Sph.” 239b (but ὁ ἐ.}} **the Self, the Ego**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 444)}}; τίς ὢν οὗτος ὁ ἐγὼ τυγχάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1119a; τί ἐστι φίλο; ἄλλος ἐ. 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.166 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}; τί τοῦτ᾽ ἐμο; ἡμῖν τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ; Latin _quid mea hoc refert_ ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 498, etc.}}; ἐγ; in a question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1336, +others}}; ἡμεῖς **the self**, ἔνθα δὴ ἡμεῖς μάλιστα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.1.7.}}

1699:
** ἐμός**, ή, όν,
_possessive_ _pronoun_ of _1st pers._: (ἐγώ, ἐμοῦ):— **mine**; _contraction_ with the _Article_, οὑμός, τοὐμόν, τοὐμοῦ, τὠμῷ, τἀμά, Trag. (not Comedy texts, τἀμὰ γὰρ διοίχεται is _paratragoedia_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 393)}}, rarely in Prose, οὑμός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 354c; τἀμά [prev. author] “Plt.” 258b}}; οὑμός even in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.360}}; and (_accusative_ to some Grammars ) τὠμῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|11.608, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.71; τἠμῇ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.654}}:—_poetry_ **ἀμός** (which see):
**I** with a _substantive_:
**I.1** subjectively, **mine, of me**, ἐμὰ δάκρυα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.42}}; χεῖρες ἐμαί{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 166; ἐμός τε πατὴρ καὶ σός 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 633}}: with the _Article_, τὸν ἐμὸν χόλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.42, etc.}}: in Poets sometimes joined with _genitive_, to strengthen the **possessive** notion, ἐμὸν αὐτοῦ **mine** own,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|6.446, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.45; δαὴρ.. ἐμὸς ἔσκε κυνώπιδος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.180; θρῆνον ἐμὸν τὸν αὐτῆς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1323; τἀμὰ δυστήνου κακά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 344, compare “El.” 252; τὸν ἐμὸν αὐτοῦ.. βίον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 33.}}
**I.1.b** **mine**, i.e. **favourable to me**, τεκμήρια ἐμά, οὐ τούτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.10.}}
**I.2** objectively, **relating to me, against me**, ἐμὴ ἀγγελίη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.336; τὸν ἐμὸν γάμον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.97}}; τὴν ἐμὴν αἰδῶ respect **for me**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 699 (troch.)}}; τἀμὰ νουθετήματα warnings **to me**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 343}}; τὠμῷ πόθῳ by love **for me**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 969}}; αἱ ἐμαὶ διαβολαί slanders **against me**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.90}}; δωρεὰ ἐμή a gift **to me**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.32}}; sometimes with another _genitive_ added, τὰς ἐμὰς Λαΐου διαφθοράς murder of L. **by me**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 572}}; τοὐμὸν αἷμα πατρός his blood shed **by me**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1400}}; τὰ ἐμὰ δῶρα Κύπριδος (Dind. for Κύπρις) her gifts **to me**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 364 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II** without a _substantive_, **mine**, οὐ γὰρ ἐμὸν παλινάγρετον **my word**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.526}}; τὸ μὲν ἐμόν [ἐστι] 'tis **my counsel**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).42}}: in Trag. and Prose, it is **my duty, my business**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1020, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 664b.}}
**II.2** ἐμοί **my friends**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.205; οἱ ἐμοί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.28, etc.}}; ὁ ἐμὸς Ἡράκλειτος **my dear** Heraclitus, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.2.17.}}
**II.3** τὰ ἐμά **my property**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e, etc.}}; of children, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 538, “OC” 922; τὸ τύπτειν καὶ ἐμὲ καὶ τὰ ἐμὰ ἀδίκως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 508e}}; of servants. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEdgar” 4.6 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; but also τὰ ἐμά or τὸ ἐμόν, **my part, my affairs, my interest**, οὕτω τὸ ἐμὸν ἔχει things stand thus **with me**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.127; τὰ τούτου μᾶλλον ἢ τοὐμόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 124; ἔρρει τἀμὰ παντελῶς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.3; τὸ ἐμὸν εὖ πράττει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 463e, etc.; in full, τοὐμὸν μέρος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1215}}: hence in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, **my conduct** (almost _periphrastic_ for ἐγώ), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 1302, “Tr.” 1068, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 105; τὸ μὲν οὖν ἐμὸν οὐκ ἐμποδὼν ὑμῖν ἔσται 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 8.19, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 452c, etc.: _absolutely_, τό γε ἐμόν}} **for my part, as far as concerns me**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 338c, “Sph.” 237b.}}
**II.4** ἡ ἐ. (i.e. γῆ) **my country**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.78}}; also (i.e. γνώμη) **my opinion**, ἐὰν ἡ ἐ. νικᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 397d}}; κατά γε τὴν ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 153, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 277a.}}
